#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:5
msgid "The package `Luna-Flow/geometry3d/backend/canvas` draws a frontend `DrawList` on an HTML `<canvas>` with the 2D context. It rasterizes the list into the frontend's software depth buffer, quantizes each pixel's intensity to a shade of one foreground colour, and paints horizontal runs of equal shade with `fillRect`. It supports only the `js` target and uses the DOM bindings of `moonbit-community/rabbita`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:7
#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:7
#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:7
#: manual/api/core.md:7
#: manual/api/frontend.md:7
#: manual/api/view.md:9
msgid "Importing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:19
msgid "Add `moonbit-community/rabbita` to your module (`moon add moonbit-community/rabbita@0.12.4`). The [Canvas design](../../design/backend/canvas.md) explains the shading and the run merging; the [Canvas tutorial](../../tutorial/backend/canvas.md) builds a page around it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:21
#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:21
#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:19
msgid "Configuration"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:25
msgid "`CanvasColor` is an RGB colour with channels in $[0, 255]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:37
msgid "`CanvasColor::rgb(r, g, b)` builds a colour, clamping each channel to $[0, 255]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:45
msgid "`CanvasRenderConfig` is the canvas size, the background colour, the foreground colour and the number of shade levels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:57
msgid "The fields are read-only outside the package, so a configuration comes from the constructors below. Every render call normalizes it again: non-positive sizes become 640 × 480, channels are clamped, and `shade_levels` is clamped to $[2, 256]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:61
msgid "`CanvasRenderConfig::default()` is 640 × 480; `CanvasRenderConfig::sized(w, h)` uses the given size (non-positive values become 640 or 480). Both use the background `rgb(7, 12, 22)`, the foreground `rgb(112, 226, 255)` and 256 shade levels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:68
#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:71
msgid "`default` is an ordinary constructor, not an implementation of the `Default` trait."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:79
#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:77
#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:73
msgid "Rendering"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:83
msgid "`render_draw_list(context, list, config)` paints a draw list on a 2D context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:89
msgid "It fills the whole configured area with the background colour, rasterizes the list with `@frontend.draw_list_to_luma` at the configured size, and for each covered pixel computes the shade $q = \\operatorname{round}(\\operatorname{clamp}(I)\\,(L - 1))$ with $L$ shade levels. It then paints each maximal horizontal run of covered pixels with equal $q$ as one 1-pixel-high `fillRect` in the colour $\\operatorname{round}(\\mathit{fg} \\cdot q / (L - 1))$. The fill style is set only when the shade changes between consecutive runs. A covered pixel with intensity 0 is painted black, not with the background colour. The draw list should have been projected into a viewport of the configured size."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:93
msgid "`render_scene(context, scene, view, config)` runs `@frontend.build_draw_list` and `render_draw_list`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:101
msgid "`render_canvas(canvas, list, config)` sets the canvas element's `width` and `height` attributes to the configured size, gets its 2D context and calls `render_draw_list`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/canvas.md:107
msgid "It aborts if the element has no 2D context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:1
msgid "backend/gsap API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:5
msgid "The package `Luna-Flow/geometry3d/backend/gsap` draws a frontend `DrawList` as SVG polygons and drives animations with a [GSAP](https://gsap.com/) timeline. Triangles are ordered far to near (painter's algorithm) and written into reusable `<polygon>` nodes of an `<svg>` element. `GsapPlayer` wraps a GSAP timeline that calls back into MoonBit with the current time. The package supports only the `js` target, uses `moonbit-community/rabbita/dom`, and expects GSAP 3 at `globalThis.gsap`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:19
msgid "The [GSAP design](../../design/backend/gsap.md) explains the ordering and its limits; the [GSAP tutorial](../../tutorial/backend/gsap.md) builds a player page."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:25
msgid "`GsapSvgColor` is an RGB colour with channels in $[0, 255]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:37
msgid "`GsapSvgColor::rgb(r, g, b)` builds a colour, clamping each channel to $[0, 255]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:45
msgid "`GsapSvgRenderConfig` is the SVG size, the background colour, the foreground colour and the number of shade levels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:57
msgid "The fields are read-only outside the package. Each render call normalizes the configuration: non-positive sizes become 640 × 480, channels are clamped, and `shade_levels` is clamped to $[2, 256]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:61
msgid "`GsapSvgRenderConfig::default()` is 640 × 480 and `GsapSvgRenderConfig::sized(w, h)` uses the given size (non-positive values become 640 or 480). Both use the background `rgb(7, 12, 22)`, the foreground `rgb(112, 226, 255)` and 256 shade levels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:81
msgid "`render_draw_list(svg, list, config)` updates an `<svg>` element to show a draw list."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:87
msgid "It sorts the triangles by the mean depth of their three vertices, farthest first, keeping the list order among equal depths. It sets the element's `width`, `height`, `viewBox` (`0 0 W H`), `role=\"img\"` and `data-geometry3d-backend=\"gsap-svg\"` attributes. It makes sure the element has a background `<rect data-gsap-svg-background>` and a group `<g data-gsap-svg-triangles>` as its direct children (replacing all children the first time). It then grows or shrinks the group to one `<polygon>` per triangle and writes each polygon's `points` and `fill`. The fill is the foreground colour scaled by the quantized intensity $q/(L - 1)$, $q = \\operatorname{round}(\\operatorname{clamp}(I)(L - 1))$, as in the Canvas backend. Polygons are reused between calls, so repeated rendering does not recreate DOM nodes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:91
msgid "`render_scene(svg, scene, view, config)` runs `@frontend.build_draw_list` and `render_draw_list`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:112
msgid "Playback"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:116
msgid "`GsapTimeline` is an opaque handle to a JavaScript GSAP timeline object."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:124
msgid "`GsapPlayer` is a paused-by-default GSAP timeline of a fixed duration that reports its time to a MoonBit callback."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:135
msgid "`GsapPlayer::new(duration, on_frame, repeat?)` creates a paused timeline that tweens a clock from $0$ to `duration` seconds with a linear ease and calls `on_frame(t)` on every update. It also calls `on_frame(0.0)` once immediately."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:141
msgid "A non-positive duration becomes 1 s. `repeat` is GSAP's repeat count: `-1` (the default) repeats forever, `0` plays once. It aborts with a JavaScript error if `globalThis.gsap.timeline` is not available."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:145
msgid "These methods forward to the timeline's `play()`, `pause()`, `reverse()`, `restart()` and `kill()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:155
msgid "After `kill` the player must not be used again."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:159
msgid "`player.seek(t)` moves the playhead to `t` seconds, clamped to $[0, \\mathit{duration}]$; `player.time()` returns the playhead's local time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:168
msgid "`player.progress()` returns the playhead position as a fraction of one iteration; `player.set_progress(p)` sets it, clamping `p` to $[0, 1]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:177
msgid "`player.time_scale()` returns the playback speed factor; `player.set_time_scale(k)` sets it, replacing a non-positive `k` by `1.0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:184
msgid "Use `reverse` rather than a negative scale to play backwards."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:188
msgid "`player.set_repeat(n)` sets the repeat count, raising values below `-1` to `-1` (repeat forever)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/gsap.md:196
msgid "`player.is_paused()` returns whether the timeline is paused."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:1
msgid "backend/tui API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:5
msgid "The package `Luna-Flow/geometry3d/backend/tui` draws a frontend `DrawList` as text: a character frame buffer with a depth buffer, a shade ramp that maps intensity to characters, background patterns, the correction for tall terminal cells, and plain-text file formats for single images (`.tuiimg`) and animated sequences (`.tui3d`). It runs on every target and produces strings; printing them is up to the caller."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:17
msgid "The [TUI design](../../design/backend/tui.md) explains the aspect correction and the quantization; the [TUI tutorial](../../tutorial/backend/tui.md) renders scenes step by step."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:23
msgid "The fallback output size, 80 columns by 32 rows."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:32
msgid "The default vertical squeeze, `0.5`, for terminal cells about twice as tall as they are wide."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:40
msgid "The default ramp `\" .:-=+*#%@\"`, ten characters from darkest to brightest."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:48
msgid "`TuiRenderConfig` is the output size, the vertical squeeze, the shade ramp and the background pattern."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:60
msgid "The fields are read-only outside the package; obtain a configuration from the two constructors below. To draw with another ramp or background, use `FrameBuffer`, `shade_char` and `draw_triangle_z` directly, as shown in the tutorial."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:64
msgid "`TuiRenderConfig::default()` is 80 × 32 with the default scale, ramp and `dotted_background`; `TuiRenderConfig::sized(w, h)` is the same with the given size, replacing a non-positive dimension by the default."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:77
msgid "`render_draw_list(list, config)` creates a frame buffer filled with the background pattern and draws every triangle of the list: it squeezes the vertices vertically, picks the character for the triangle's intensity, and rasterizes with the depth test."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:83
msgid "The draw list should have been projected into a viewport of the same size as the configuration. A triangle of intensity 0 is drawn with the first ramp character (a space by default), so unlit faces still hide what is behind them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:87
msgid "`render_frame(list, config)` is `render_draw_list(list, config).to_string()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:95
msgid "`render_scene(scene, view, config)` runs `build_draw_list` and `render_draw_list` in one call."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:133
msgid "`draw_list_to_tui_luma(list, config)` rasterizes the list into a frontend `LumaBuffer` of the configured size, applying the same vertical squeeze. Use it to accumulate exposures before turning them into characters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:141
msgid "`render_luma_buffer(buffer, config)` turns a luma buffer into a frame buffer: every pixel with a value above `0.0` gets the ramp character for its value and keeps its depth; other pixels show the background."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:147
msgid "Only the overlap of the buffer and the configured size is used."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:151
msgid "`render_luma_frame(buffer, config)` is `render_luma_buffer(buffer, config).to_string()`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:183
msgid "Frame buffer"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:187
msgid "`FAR_DEPTH` is the depth of a background cell, $10^{30}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:195
msgid "`FrameBuffer` is a row-major grid of characters with a depth per cell."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:208
msgid "`FrameBuffer::new(width, height, pattern)` fills every cell $(x, y)$ with `pattern(x, y, width, height)` at depth `FAR_DEPTH`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:216
msgid "`buffer.index(x, y)` returns `y * width + x`, without bounds checks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:224
msgid "`buffer.set_pixel_if_closer(x, y, depth, ch)` writes `ch` if `depth + DEPTH_EPSILON` is less than the stored depth; writes outside the buffer are ignored."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:232
msgid ""
"`buffer.to_string()` returns the rows joined, each followed by `'\\n"
"'`; the result has `height * (width + 1)` characters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:238
msgid "It is a method, not an implementation of `Show`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:250
msgid "Backgrounds"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:254
msgid "These patterns fill every cell with `'.'`, with `' '`, or alternately with `'.'` and `' '` when $x + y$ is even or odd."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:262
msgid "A pattern is any function `(x, y, width, height) -> Char`, so you can write your own."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:274
msgid "Rasterization helpers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:278
msgid "`apply_terminal_y_scale(p, height, k)` squeezes a projected vertex towards the middle row: $y' = H/2 + (y - H/2)\\,k$; `x` and `depth` are unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:286
msgid "`clamp01(v)` clamps a number to $[0, 1]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:294
msgid "`shade_char(ramp, intensity)` returns the character at index $\\operatorname{round}(\\operatorname{clamp01}(I) \\cdot (n - 1))$ of a ramp of $n$ characters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:300
msgid "The ramp must not be empty, and should consist of characters from the Basic Multilingual Plane (one UTF-16 unit each)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:304
msgid "`edge_function(ax, ay, bx, by, px, py)` returns $(p_x - a_x)(b_y - a_y) - (p_y - a_y)(b_x - a_x)$, the signed doubled area that decides on which side of the line $ab$ the point $p$ lies."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:312
msgid "`draw_triangle_z(buffer, p0, p1, p2, ch)` rasterizes one triangle into a frame buffer with character `ch`, sampling cell centres and using the perspective-correct depth for the depth test."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:318
msgid "Either winding is accepted; triangles with an area of at most `DEPTH_EPSILON` are skipped. Vertices are used as given, without the vertical squeeze."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:340
msgid "Images and sequences"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:344
msgid "`TuiImage` is one rendered frame with its size."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:356
msgid "`TuiImage::new(width, height, content)` builds an image, raising a dimension below 1 to 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:364
msgid "`encode_tui_image(image)` writes the `.tuiimg` text format; `decode_tui_image(text)` reads it back."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:371
msgid "The format is the line `GEOMETRY3D_TUI_IMAGE v1`, the lines `width=W` and `height=H`, the line `---image---`, and the content, terminated by a newline. The decoder reads at most `H` content lines and drops `'\\r'`. It never fails: unreadable numbers fall back to the defaults, and a missing magic line gives an empty 80 × 32 image."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:375
msgid "`TuiFrame` is one frame of a sequence."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:385
msgid "`TuiFrame::new(content)` wraps a frame."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:393
msgid "`TuiSequence` is an animation: size, frame rate and frames."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:406
msgid "`TuiSequence::new(width, height, fps)` builds an empty sequence, raising values below 1 to 1; `sequence.push_frame(content)` appends a frame in place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:415
msgid "`encode_tui_sequence(sequence)` writes the `.tui3d` text format; `decode_tui_sequence(text)` reads it back."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/backend/tui.md:422
msgid "The format is the line `GEOMETRY3D_TUI_SEQUENCE v1`, the lines `width=W`, `height=H`, `fps=F` and `frames=N`, and for each frame the line `---frame---` followed by its content and a newline. The decoder takes up to `H` lines after each marker as a frame and ignores the `frames=` count. A missing magic line gives an empty 80 × 32 sequence at 30 fps."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:1
msgid "core API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:5
msgid "The package `Luna-Flow/geometry3d/core` holds the 3D data and the affine math of the pipeline: vectors, quad meshes, mesh generators, face normals, back-face visibility, Lambert intensity and 4×4 homogeneous transforms. It knows nothing about cameras, screens, terminals or the browser. Vectors and matrices are the mutable dense types of `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra`, imported here as `@la`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:9
msgid "Import it with:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:18
msgid "The [core design](../design/core.md) derives the formulas used below, and the [core tutorial](../tutorial/core.md) walks through them with examples."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:20
msgid "Constants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:24
msgid "`DEPTH_EPSILON` is the shared tolerance of the whole pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:30
msgid "It is used as the length below which `normalize_vec` returns the zero vector, the size of `w` below which `Transform3::apply_point` skips the homogeneous division, the minimum triangle area for rasterization, and the margin of every depth test in the `view`, `frontend` and backend packages."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:32
msgid "Vectors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:34
msgid "All vector helpers take and return `@la.Vector[Double]`. They read only the components they need (indices `0..2` for 3D helpers), so a 4-component vector passed to a 3D helper is treated as its first three components."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:38
msgid "`vec3` builds the 3-component vector $(x, y, z)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:46
msgid "`vec4` builds the 4-component vector $(x, y, z, w)$, the homogeneous form used inside `Transform3`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:54
msgid "`sub_vec(a, b)` returns the 3D difference $a - b$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:62
msgid "`cross_vec(a, b)` returns the cross product $a \\times b = (a_y b_z - a_z b_y,\\ a_z b_x - a_x b_z,\\ a_x b_y - a_y b_x)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:70
msgid "`vec_length(v)` returns the Euclidean norm $\\lVert v \\rVert = \\sqrt{v \\cdot v}$ over all components of `v`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:76
msgid "Do not confuse it with `@la.Vector::length`, which returns the number of components."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:80
msgid "`normalize_vec(v)` returns $v / \\lVert v \\rVert$, or the zero vector $(0, 0, 0)$ when $\\lVert v \\rVert \\le$ `DEPTH_EPSILON`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:86
msgid "The zero result makes degenerate faces harmless: their normal is zero, so they are never visible and never lit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:103
msgid "`min3` and `max3` return the smallest and largest of three numbers. The rasterizers use them for triangle bounding boxes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:117
msgid "Meshes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:121
msgid "`QuadFace` is one face of a mesh, given by four indices into the vertex array."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:132
msgid "The vertices are listed so that $(b - a) \\times (c - a)$ points out of a closed mesh. A triangle is stored as a degenerate quad whose last index repeats the first ($d = a$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:136
msgid "`TriangleFace` is a triangle given by three vertex indices; `triangulate_quad` produces it for the rasterizers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:148
msgid "`Mesh` is an indexed face set: vertex positions plus quad faces that refer to them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:157
msgid "The fields of `Mesh`, `QuadFace` and `TriangleFace` are read-only outside the package, so you obtain meshes from the generators below and change them with `Transform3::apply_mesh`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:161
msgid "These generators build the closed primitive meshes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:163
msgid "Every generator returns a closed mesh with planar faces, centred on the origin, and clamps its resolution arguments to at least 3. `cube_mesh`, `sphere_mesh` and `torus_mesh` wind every face outwards: $(b - a) \\times (c - a)$ points out of the solid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:165
msgid "[!WARNING] `cylinder_mesh`, `cone_mesh` and `triangular_pyramid_mesh` wind every face the other way, so their face normals point into the solid. `face_is_visible` then keeps the far side of the solid instead of the near side, and `face_intensity` lights the inner surface: a cylinder seen from above shows its bottom cap from the inside and no top cap, and the shading of the walls is mirrored. Until the generators are fixed, apply a reflection that maps the mesh onto itself, because a reflection reverses the winding (see the [core design](../design/core.md#normals-recomputed-not-transformed)): `Transform3::scale(1.0, 1.0, -1.0)` for cylinders and cones, `Transform3::scale(-1.0, 1.0, 1.0)` for the pyramid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:180
msgid "Generator"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:180
msgid "Shape"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:180
msgid "Vertices"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:180
#: manual/api/core.md:245
msgid "Faces"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:182
msgid "cube $[-s, s]^3$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:183
msgid "UV sphere of radius $r$, poles on the $y$ axis"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:184
msgid "capped cylinder along $y$, $y \\in [-h/2, h/2]$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:185
msgid "capped cone, apex at $y = h/2$, base at $y = -h/2$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:186
msgid "tetrahedron with a base triangle inscribed in a circle of radius $r$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:187
msgid "torus around the $y$ axis"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:198
msgid "Caps, cone sides and pyramid faces are degenerate quads ($d = a$). `torus_mesh` also replaces a non-positive major radius by `1.0` and a non-positive minor radius by `0.25`; the other generators use their size arguments as given."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:216
msgid "`face_vertices(mesh, face)` returns the four vertices of `face`, in the order `a`, `b`, `c`, `d`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:222
msgid "The vectors are shared with `mesh.vertices`, not copied."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:226
msgid "`triangulate_quad(face)` splits a quad into the fan $(a, b, c)$, $(a, c, d)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:232
msgid "Both triangles keep the winding of the quad. For a degenerate quad the second triangle $(a, c, a)$ has zero area and the rasterizers skip it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:247
msgid "The face helpers take the vertex array separately from the face, so you can pass vertices that were already transformed into another space (for example camera space) while reusing the face list of the original mesh."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:251
msgid "`face_center(vertices, face)` returns the mean of the four face vertices, $\\tfrac14(v_a + v_b + v_c + v_d)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:257
msgid "For a degenerate quad the repeated vertex is counted twice."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:261
msgid "`face_normal(vertices, face)` returns the unit normal $\\hat n = \\operatorname{normalize}((v_b - v_a) \\times (v_c - v_a))$, computed from the first three vertices."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:267
msgid "It returns the zero vector when the three vertices are collinear."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:271
msgid "`face_is_visible(vertices, face, eye)` is the back-face test: it returns `true` when $\\hat n \\cdot (e - c) > 0$, where $c$ is the face centre."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:279
msgid "`face_intensity(vertices, face, light)` returns the Lambert term $\\max(0, \\hat n \\cdot \\ell)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:285
msgid "`light` should be a unit vector pointing from the surface towards the light; the result is then the cosine of the angle of incidence, in $[0, 1]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:303
msgid "Matrices"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:305
msgid "The matrix builders return 4×4 `@la.Matrix[Double]` values that act on column vectors $(x, y, z, w)^\\mathsf{T}$. Angles are in radians."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:309
msgid "`identity_matrix4()` returns the 4×4 identity matrix $I_4$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:317
msgid "`translation_matrix(x, y, z)` returns $\\begin{pmatrix} I_3 & t \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$ with $t = (x, y, z)^\\mathsf{T}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:325
msgid "`scale_matrix(x, y, z)` returns $\\operatorname{diag}(x, y, z, 1)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:333
msgid "`rotation_x(θ)`, `rotation_y(θ)` and `rotation_z(θ)` return the rotations by $\\theta$ about the coordinate axes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:341
msgid "Their 3×3 blocks are"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:349
msgid "A positive angle turns $y$ towards $z$, $z$ towards $x$, and $x$ towards $y$ respectively."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:353
msgid "`rotation_matrix(α, β, γ)` returns the Euler-angle rotation $R_z(\\gamma)\\,R_y(\\beta)\\,R_x(\\alpha)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:359
msgid "Applied to a column vector, the rotation about $x$ acts first, then $y$, then $z$, all about the fixed world axes. The [core design](../design/core.md) gives the full matrix and its gimbal-lock behaviour."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:374
msgid "Transforms"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:378
msgid "`Transform3` wraps one 4×4 homogeneous matrix and applies it to points, directions and meshes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:388
msgid "These constructors wrap `identity_matrix4`, `translation_matrix`, `scale_matrix` and `rotation_matrix`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:399
msgid "`Transform3::rotation_euler(α, β, γ)` is the same as `Transform3::rotation(α, β, γ)`; the name states the angle convention."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:407
msgid "`Transform3::from_matrix(m)` wraps any 4×4 matrix, including projective ones whose last row is not $(0, 0, 0, 1)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:413
msgid "The matrix is stored, not copied, and must be 4×4: the apply methods abort on other shapes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:417
msgid "`t.compose(next)` returns the transform that applies `t` first and `next` second; its matrix is `next.matrix * t.matrix`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:423
msgid "Composition is associative but not commutative. Read a chain from left to right: `scale.compose(rotation).compose(translation)` scales, then rotates, then translates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:427
msgid "`t.apply_point(p)` maps the point $p$ through $M (p_x, p_y, p_z, 1)^\\mathsf{T} = (x', y', z', w')^\\mathsf{T}$ and returns $(x'/w', y'/w', z'/w')$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:433
msgid "For affine transforms $w' = 1$. When $|w'| \\le$ `DEPTH_EPSILON` the division is skipped and $(x', y', z')$ is returned."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:437
msgid "`t.apply_direction(d)` maps the direction $d$ with $w = 0$, so translations do not affect it, and returns the first three components without division."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:443
msgid "The result is not renormalized. Normals are not transformed this way in the pipeline: they are recomputed from transformed vertices."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:447
msgid "`t.apply_vertex(v)` is the same as `t.apply_point(v)`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:455
msgid "`t.apply_mesh(mesh)` returns a new mesh with every vertex mapped by `apply_point` and the same face array."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/core.md:461
msgid "The face array is shared with the input mesh, which is safe because faces are immutable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:1
msgid "demo API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:5
msgid "`Luna-Flow/geometry3d/demo` is an executable, not a library: it exports no MoonBit items. Its interface is the command line described here. It renders animated scenes into the terminal with the [TUI backend](backend/tui.md), and records, plays, exports and shows `.tui3d` sequences and `.tuiimg` images. It is meant for the `native` target, which provides the clock, the environment and the file system it uses."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:11
msgid "Options are matched exactly; unknown options are ignored. The [demo tutorial](../tutorial/demo.md) shows typical sessions and the [demo design](../design/demo.md) explains the scenes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:13
msgid "Environment"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:17
msgid "The frame is `COLUMNS` cells wide and `LINES - 1` rows high; one row is left for the shell prompt. Missing or unreadable values give 80 × 32. The `just` recipes of the repository set both from `stty size`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:19
#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:26
#: manual/api/frontend.md:20
msgid "Scenes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:21
msgid "Without a scene option the demo spins a cube of half-size 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:25
msgid "`--sphere` shows a static UV sphere of radius 2.4 with 18 rings and 36 segments."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:29
msgid "`--torus` spins a torus with radii 2.1 and 0.72 and 28 × 16 segments, seen from 6.8 units away."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:33
msgid "`--hitchcock` shows a central cube in front of cylinders, cones and a triangular pyramid while the camera dollies between 3.4 and 8 units away and the focal length follows the distance, so the cube keeps its size and the background changes scale."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:37
msgid "`--dolly` shows a spinning cube in front of a wall of 5 × 7 discs while the camera dollies between 3.2 and about 69.6 units and the focal length grows from 23 mm to 500 mm."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:39
msgid "The mesh scenes (cube, sphere, torus) use an 18 mm lens on a full-frame sensor, 4.5 units away unless stated otherwise."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:41
msgid "Effects"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:45
msgid "`--long-exposure` averages 12 renders of the preceding motion into each frame, which blurs moving edges."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:49
msgid "`--flow-exposure` is `--long-exposure` with each earlier render first aligned to the current one by block-matching optical flow (search radius 4, patch radius 1)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:51
msgid "Running"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:55
msgid "`--once` renders a single frame (or plays a single frame of a sequence) and exits. Without it, the live modes run until interrupted, at one frame per 33 ms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:57
msgid "Files"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:61
msgid "`--record PATH` renders a whole sequence for the selected scene and effects and writes it to `PATH` as `.tui3d`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:65
msgid "`--record-stdout` writes the same `.tui3d` text to standard output frame by frame, without keeping the sequence in memory. Redirect it to a file for long recordings."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:69
msgid "These options set the length (default 3 s) and frame rate (default 30) of `--record` and `--record-stdout`. Both take positive integers: the parser keeps only the digits of the argument, so `--duration 0.5` means 5 seconds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:73
msgid "`--play PATH` reads a `.tui3d` file and plays it in the terminal at its recorded frame rate (the nearest of 60, 30, 24, 12 or 10 frames per second, rounded down)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:77
msgid "`--export-image PATH` renders one still of the selected scene and effects and writes it to `PATH` as `.tuiimg`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:81
msgid "`--show-image PATH` reads a `.tuiimg` file and prints it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:83
msgid "Precedence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo.md:85
msgid "When several modes are given, the first that applies wins, in this order: `--record-stdout`, `--show-image`, `--export-image`, `--play`, `--record`, `--dolly`, `--hitchcock`, then the mesh scenes. A file that cannot be read or written aborts the program with a message naming the path."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:1
msgid "demo_canvas API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:5
msgid "`Luna-Flow/geometry3d/demo_canvas` is a browser executable for the `js` target. It exports no MoonBit items; its interface is the HTML page it expects and the scenes it draws with the [Canvas backend](backend/canvas.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:7
#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:7
msgid "Build and serve"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:14
msgid "`canvas-build` copies `src/demo_canvas/index.html` and the compiled `demo_canvas.js` (as `demo.js`) into `target/canvas-demo/`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:16
#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:16
msgid "Page contract"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:20
msgid "The page must contain a `<canvas id=\"geometry3d-canvas\">`. The program sets its `width` and `height` attributes to 640 × 480 and draws into its 2D context. The program aborts at start-up if the element or the context is missing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:24
msgid "The page must contain a `<select id=\"demo-select\">`. Its value `dolly` selects the dolly-zoom scene; any other value selects the torus. The program reads the value at start-up and on every `change` event."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:28
#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:44
msgid "Torus"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:30
msgid "The torus scene shows a torus with radii 1.7 and 0.58 (32 × 18 segments), turning by $0.0006$ rad per millisecond of the animation timestamp about $x$, $y$ and $z$ in the ratio $0.7 : 1 : 0.25$. The camera is 6.2 units away with a 35 mm lens on a full-frame sensor."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:32
#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:48
#: manual/design/view.md:153
msgid "Dolly zoom"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_canvas.md:34
msgid "The dolly scene is the scene of the terminal demo's `--dolly` mode, with the frame number derived from the animation timestamp at 30 frames per second."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:1
msgid "demo_gsap API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:5
msgid "`Luna-Flow/geometry3d/demo_gsap` is a browser executable for the `js` target. It exports no MoonBit items; its interface is the HTML page it expects and the player it builds with the [GSAP SVG backend](backend/gsap.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:14
msgid "`gsap-build` copies `src/demo_gsap/index.html` and the compiled `demo_gsap.js` (as `demo.js`) into `target/gsap-demo/`. The page loads GSAP 3.13.0 from jsDelivr, sets `globalThis.gsap`, and then imports `demo.js`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:20
msgid "The `<svg id=\"geometry3d-svg\">` element receives the rendered polygons, 640 × 480."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:24
msgid "These buttons pause or resume, reverse, and restart the timeline. The program sets the text of `play-toggle` to `PLAY` or `PAUSE`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:28
msgid "The range input `progress` (0 to 1) shows and seeks the timeline position; the element `time-readout` shows the time as `t / 8.00s`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:32
msgid "The selector chooses the scene: the value `dolly` selects the dolly zoom, any other value the torus."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:36
msgid "The `speed` selector sets the playback speed factor, and the `loop` checkbox switches between endless repetition and a single run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:38
msgid "The program aborts at start-up if one of these elements is missing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:40
msgid "Timeline"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:42
msgid "The timeline is 8 seconds long and repeats forever by default. Every update renders the selected scene for the current time $t$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:46
msgid "The torus scene turns the torus of the Canvas demo by $1.8\\,t$ radians about $y$ (with $0.7$ and $0.25$ of that about $x$ and $z$), from 6.2 units away with a 35 mm lens."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/demo_gsap.md:50
msgid "The dolly scene uses the camera distance $d(t) = 3.2 + 66.37 \\cdot \\tfrac12\\big(1 + \\sin(\\pi t / 2)\\big)$, so it completes two dolly cycles per 8-second loop. The focal length is $23 \\cdot d / 3.2$ mm, and the cube turns at $1.23$, $1.71$ and $0.69$ rad/s."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:1
msgid "frontend API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:5
msgid "The package `Luna-Flow/geometry3d/frontend` turns a scene and a camera into a backend-neutral list of shaded, projected triangles (`DrawList`). It also owns the software depth buffer for scalar images (`LumaBuffer`) used by the Canvas backend and the exposure effects, a block-matching optical-flow estimator, exposure settings and animation timelines. It knows nothing about characters, colours or the DOM."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:18
msgid "The [frontend design](../design/frontend.md) explains the pipeline, the shadow map and the rasterization rule; the [frontend tutorial](../tutorial/frontend.md) shows them in use."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:24
msgid "`SceneObject` is one mesh with its model transform."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:35
msgid "`SceneObject::new(mesh, transform)` builds an object; the same mesh may be shared by several objects."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:43
msgid "`Light` is a directional light: `direction` is the unit vector from the scene towards the light, in world space."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:53
msgid "`Light::directional(direction)` builds a light, normalizing `direction`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:59
msgid "A zero vector gives a light that illuminates nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:63
msgid "`Light::default()` is `Light::directional((0.6, 0.7, -1.0))`: from the upper right, on the side of the default camera."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:69
#: manual/api/view.md:51
msgid "It is an ordinary constructor, not an implementation of the `Default` trait."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:73
msgid "`Scene` is a list of objects lit by one directional light."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:84
msgid "`Scene::new(light)` builds an empty scene; `Scene::single(mesh, transform, light)` builds a scene with one object."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:93
msgid "`scene.add_object(object)` appends an object to the scene in place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:116
msgid "Render views and draw lists"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:120
msgid "`RenderView` is the camera and the perspective projection used to render a scene."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:131
msgid "`RenderView::perspective(camera, projection)` pairs a camera with a projection; `RenderView::scientific(camera, viewport)` takes the camera of a `ScientificCamera` and derives the projection from its lens and sensor."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:140
msgid "`DrawTriangle` is one projected triangle with a flat intensity, nominally in $[0, 1]$; rounding can push it a few ulps above 1, and backends clamp it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:151
msgid "The vertices are in viewport units with camera-space depth, as produced by `PerspectiveProjection`. The winding on the screen is not normalized; rasterizers accept both orientations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:155
msgid "`DrawTriangle::new(p0, p1, p2, intensity)` builds a triangle, for example to drive a backend without a scene."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:163
msgid "`DrawList` is the ordered list of triangles that every backend consumes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:173
msgid "`DrawList::new()` builds an empty list and `list.push_triangle(t)` appends a triangle in place."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:182
msgid "`build_draw_list(scene, view)` runs the geometric pipeline: model transform, shadow map, view transform, projection, back-face culling, Lambert shading with shadows, and triangulation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:188
msgid "For every face that faces the camera it pushes two triangles (the second has zero area for a degenerate quad) with the intensity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:194
msgid "where $v$ is the fraction of the face's centre and four vertices that the light reaches according to a 128 × 128 shadow map. Faces turned away from the light get $I = 0$. Triangles appear object by object in scene order, and face by face within an object; the list is not depth-sorted. The cost is linear in the number of vertices and faces plus the rasterization of the shadow map."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:214
msgid "Luma buffers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:218
msgid "`LUMA_FAR_DEPTH` is the depth of an empty pixel, $10^{30}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:224
msgid "A pixel whose depth is at least `LUMA_FAR_DEPTH * 0.5` is treated as background by the backends."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:228
msgid "`LumaBuffer` is a row-major image of scalar luma values with a depth per pixel."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:241
msgid "`LumaBuffer::new(width, height)` builds a buffer with every value `0.0` and every depth `LUMA_FAR_DEPTH`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:249
msgid "`buffer.index(x, y)` returns the array index `y * width + x`, without bounds checks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:257
msgid "`buffer.get(x, y)` returns the value at a pixel and `buffer.depth_at(x, y)` its depth; outside the buffer they return `0.0` and `LUMA_FAR_DEPTH`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:266
msgid "`buffer.set_if_closer(x, y, depth, value)` is the depth test: it writes the pixel only if `depth + DEPTH_EPSILON` is less than the stored depth."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:272
msgid "Writes outside the buffer are ignored. Equal depths keep the value written first."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:276
msgid "`buffer.draw_triangle(t)` rasterizes one triangle: every pixel whose centre $(x + \\tfrac12, y + \\tfrac12)$ lies inside or on the edge of the triangle receives `t.intensity` if it passes the depth test with the perspective-correct depth."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:282
msgid "Triangles with an area of at most `DEPTH_EPSILON` are skipped. The loop runs over the triangle's bounding box, which is not clipped to the buffer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:286
msgid "`draw_list_to_luma(list, width, height)` creates a buffer and draws every triangle of the list into it, in order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:294
msgid "`buffer.add_weighted_sample(sample, w)` adds `w * sample.values[i]` to every value in place and keeps the smaller of the two depths."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:300
msgid "Only the first $\\min$ of the two lengths is processed; use buffers of the same size."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:304
msgid "`average_luma(a, b)` returns a new buffer with the mean of the values and the minimum of the depths of two buffers of the same size."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:338
#: manual/design/demo.md:45
msgid "Exposure"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:342
msgid "`ShutterSpeed` is an exposure time in seconds."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:352
msgid "`ShutterSpeed::seconds(t)` builds a shutter of `t` seconds and `ShutterSpeed::reciprocal(n)` one of `1/n` seconds; non-positive arguments give 1/60 s."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:361
msgid "`ExposureSettings` is a shutter, the frame interval it belongs to, and the number of samples to average."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:373
msgid "`ExposureSettings::new(shutter, frame_dt, samples)` builds settings: a non-positive `frame_dt` becomes 1/60 s, the shutter is clamped to at most `frame_dt`, and `samples` is raised to at least 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:381
msgid "`ExposureSettings::auto(shutter, frame_dt)` builds settings with `samples = ceil(shutter / frame_dt)`, computed after the shutter has been clamped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:387
msgid "Because the shutter never exceeds `frame_dt` after clamping, `auto` always yields one sample. Callers that want a long exposure choose a larger count themselves, as the TUI demo does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:400
msgid "Optical flow"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:404
msgid "`FlowVector` is an integer pixel displacement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:415
msgid "`FlowVector::new(dx, dy)` builds a displacement."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:423
msgid "`FlowField` is a row-major field of displacements, one per pixel."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:435
msgid "`FlowField::new(w, h)` builds a zero field; `index` is `y * width + x`; `get` returns `(0, 0)` outside the field and `set` ignores writes outside it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:446
msgid "`estimate_optical_flow(previous, current, search_radius, patch_radius)` estimates, for every pixel of `current`, where its neighbourhood came from in `previous`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:452
msgid "For each pixel $(x, y)$ it returns the displacement $(d_x, d_y)$ with $|d_x|, |d_y| \\le$ `search_radius` that minimizes the sum of squared differences between the $(2P + 1)^2$ patch around $(x + d_x, y + d_y)$ in `previous` and the patch around $(x, y)$ in `current`, with $P$ = `patch_radius`. Pixels outside a buffer read as `0.0`. Ties keep the first displacement in the scan order ($d_y$, then $d_x$, from $-R$ upwards), so in a uniform region, where every displacement fits equally well, the result is $(-R, -R)$ rather than $(0, 0)$. Negative radii are treated as 0. The cost is $O\\big(W H (2R + 1)^2 (2P + 1)^2\\big)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:456
msgid "`align_with_flow(previous, current, flow)` warps `previous` onto the pixel grid of `current`: pixel $(x, y)$ of the result takes value and depth from $(x + d_x, y + d_y)$ in `previous`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:464
msgid "`accumulate_with_flow(previous, current, flow)` returns the average of the warped `previous` and `current`, with the depths of `current`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:488
msgid "Timelines"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:492
msgid "`Timeline` is a fixed-rate clip: a duration in seconds and a frame rate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:503
msgid "`Timeline::new(duration, fps)` builds a timeline; a non-positive duration becomes 1 s and an fps below 1 becomes 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:511
msgid "`frame_count` returns $\\max(1, \\lceil D \\cdot \\mathit{fps} \\rceil)$ and `frame_dt` returns $1/\\mathit{fps}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:520
msgid "`TimelineSample` is one frame of a timeline: its index, its time $t = k/\\mathit{fps}$ and its progress $\\min(1, t/D)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:532
msgid "`timeline.sample(k)` returns the sample of frame `k`; a negative index is treated as 0. Indices past the end are allowed and give a progress of 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:540
msgid "`ScalarKeyframe` is a value at a time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:551
msgid "`ScalarKeyframe::new(time, value)` builds a keyframe."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:559
msgid "`ScalarTrack` is a piecewise-linear animation curve through keyframes sorted by time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:569
msgid "`ScalarTrack::new(keyframes)` builds a track; the keyframes must be sorted by increasing time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:577
msgid "`track.sample(t)` returns the value at time `t`: linear interpolation between the surrounding keyframes, the first value before the first keyframe, the last value after the last one, and `0.0` for an empty track."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/frontend.md:583
msgid "When two keyframes share a time (within `DEPTH_EPSILON`), the later value wins at that time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:1
msgid "view API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:5
msgid "The package `Luna-Flow/geometry3d/view` takes points from world space to the screen. It contains the look-at camera, the perspective and orthographic projections into a viewport, the perspective-correct depth interpolation used by every rasterizer, and a physical camera model (sensor, lens, world unit) that derives the projection scale from a focal length. It does not rasterize and knows nothing about terminals or the DOM."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:7
msgid "Coordinates follow one convention throughout. In camera space $x$ points right, $y$ up and $z$ forward, so visible points have $z > 0$. On the screen $x$ grows to the right and $y$ grows downwards, in pixels (or terminal cells) of the viewport. The [view design](../design/view.md) derives every formula on this page."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:19
msgid "Camera"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:23
msgid "`Camera3` is a look-at camera: an eye position, a target point and an approximate up direction, all in world space."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:33
msgid "`up` need not be perpendicular to the viewing direction; the camera derives an orthonormal frame from it. It must not be parallel to `target - eye`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:37
msgid "`Camera3::look_at(eye, target, up)` builds a camera from its three vectors without validating them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:45
msgid "`Camera3::default(distance)` places the eye at $(0, 0, -\\mathit{distance})$, looking at the origin with up $= +y$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:55
msgid "These methods return the orthonormal camera frame."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:63
msgid "They are"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:71
msgid "$r$, $u$, $f$ are pairwise perpendicular unit vectors with $r \\times u = f$. If `up` is parallel to $f$, $r$ and $u$ are zero vectors and the camera is unusable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:75
msgid "`camera.view_transform()` returns the world-to-camera transform, the rigid motion that moves the eye to the origin and the frame $(r, u, f)$ onto the axes $(x, y, z)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:81
msgid "Its matrix has the rows $(r^\\mathsf{T}, -r \\cdot e)$, $(u^\\mathsf{T}, -u \\cdot e)$, $(f^\\mathsf{T}, -f \\cdot e)$ and $(0, 0, 0, 1)$, where $e$ is the eye."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:85
msgid "These methods apply the view transform to a point or to a direction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:92
msgid "A point becomes $(r \\cdot (p - e),\\ u \\cdot (p - e),\\ f \\cdot (p - e))$; a direction becomes $(r \\cdot d,\\ u \\cdot d,\\ f \\cdot d)$. Each call rebuilds the view matrix, so keep the transform from `view_transform` when you map many points."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:109
msgid "Viewport and projected vertices"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:113
msgid "`Viewport` is the size of the output in pixels or terminal cells."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:124
msgid "`Viewport::new(width, height)` builds a viewport without validation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:132
msgid "`ProjectedVertex` is a vertex after projection: screen coordinates `x`, `y` in viewport units, and `depth`, the camera-space $z$ of the original point."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:142
msgid "`depth` is a distance along the viewing direction, not a normalized device depth. Smaller is closer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:146
msgid "`ProjectedVertex::new(x, y, depth)` builds a projected vertex, for example to feed a rasterizer directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:152
msgid "Projections"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:156
msgid "`PerspectiveProjection` is a pinhole projection into a viewport with scale $s$ in pixels per unit of $x/z$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:167
msgid "`PerspectiveProjection::new(viewport, scale)` builds the projection. Use `ScientificCamera::to_perspective_projection` to derive `scale` from a lens."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:175
msgid "`projection.project_point(p)` maps a camera-space point to the screen:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:187
msgid "where $W \\times H$ is the viewport. The point must lie in front of the camera ($z > 0$). There is no near plane and no clipping: $z = 0$ produces infinities (or NaN for a coordinate that is also $0$) and $z < 0$ a point reflected through the centre of the viewport."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:191
msgid "`OrthographicProjection` is a parallel projection into a viewport with scale $s$ in pixels per world unit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:202
msgid "`OrthographicProjection::new(viewport, scale)` builds the projection."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:210
msgid "`projection.project_point(p)` maps a camera-space point with $x_s = W/2 + s x$, $y_s = H/2 - s y$ and $\\mathit{depth} = z$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:218
msgid "These functions project every point of an array with the given projection."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:225
msgid "The output has the same length and order as the input, so face indices of a mesh remain valid."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:247
msgid "Depth interpolation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:251
msgid "`interpolate_perspective_depth(p0, p1, p2, b0, b1, b2)` returns the depth at the screen point with barycentric coordinates $(b_0, b_1, b_2)$ in the projected triangle $p_0 p_1 p_2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:257
msgid "The result is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:263
msgid "which is the exact camera-space depth of the corresponding point on the 3D triangle when the triangle was projected with `PerspectiveProjection`. If any $z_i \\le$ `DEPTH_EPSILON`, or the sum is within `DEPTH_EPSILON` of zero, it falls back to the linear $b_0 z_0 + b_1 z_1 + b_2 z_2$. The barycentric coordinates should sum to 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:277
msgid "Physical camera"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:281
msgid "`SensorSpec` is the size of a camera sensor in millimetres."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:292
msgid "`SensorSpec::custom(width_mm, height_mm)` builds a sensor; a non-positive dimension is replaced by `1.0`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:300
msgid "These presets are 36 × 24 mm, 23.5 × 15.6 mm and 44 × 33 mm."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:310
msgid "`sensor.diagonal_mm()` returns $\\sqrt{w^2 + h^2}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:318
msgid "`LensSpec` is a lens given by its focal length in millimetres."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:328
msgid "`LensSpec::new(f)` builds a lens, replacing a non-positive focal length by `1.0`; `LensSpec::normal_full_frame()` is the 50 mm lens."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:337
msgid "These methods return the angle of view, in radians, across the sensor's width, height or diagonal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:345
msgid "Each is $2 \\arctan\\!\\big(d / (2 f)\\big)$ for the sensor dimension $d$ and the focal length $f$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:349
msgid "`WorldUnit` states how many metres one world-space unit represents."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:359
msgid "`WorldUnit::new(m)` builds the unit, replacing a non-positive value by `1.0`; `WorldUnit::unitless()` is one metre per unit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:368
msgid "`unit.millimeters_per_unit()` returns `meters_per_unit * 1000`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:376
msgid "`ScientificCamera` bundles a `Camera3` with a sensor, a lens and a world unit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:389
msgid "`ScientificCamera::new(camera, sensor, lens, world_unit)` builds the camera from its parts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:397
msgid "`ScientificCamera::auto(viewport)` returns `Camera3::default(4.5)` with a full-frame sensor, a 50 mm lens and unitless world units."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:403
msgid "The viewport argument is currently ignored."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:407
msgid "These methods return a copy with the camera or the lens replaced; they are the way to animate a dolly or a zoom."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:416
msgid "`camera.projection_scale(viewport)` returns the perspective scale $s = H f / h$, where $H$ is the viewport height, $f$ the focal length and $h$ the sensor height."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:422
msgid "With this scale the vertical angle of view spans exactly the viewport height."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:426
msgid "`camera.to_perspective_projection(viewport)` returns `PerspectiveProjection::new(viewport, camera.projection_scale(viewport))`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:434
msgid "These methods convert the focal length and the sensor height from millimetres into world units."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/view.md:441
msgid "They are informational: the projection scale depends only on the ratio $f / h$, in which the unit cancels."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:1
msgid "Architecture"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:3
msgid "This guide follows one frame through the packages of `geometry3d` and explains where each responsibility lives and why. The package pages hold the details and the derivations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:5
msgid "Layers"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:22
msgid "Each package imports only packages above it. `core`, `view` and `frontend` know nothing about characters, colours, the DOM or the operating system; each backend knows nothing about the others; and the demos are the only code that touches the clock, the environment, files or page elements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:24
msgid "One frame"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:26
msgid "**Model.** `frontend` applies each `SceneObject`'s `Transform3` to its mesh (`core`), giving world-space vertices."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:27
msgid "**Shadows.** It renders the world-space scene from the directional light into a 128 × 128 orthographic depth map."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:28
msgid "**View.** It maps vertices into camera space with the `Camera3` view transform (`view`): $x$ right, $y$ up, $z$ forward."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:29
msgid "**Projection.** It projects them with `PerspectiveProjection` into the viewport: $x_s = W/2 + s x/z$, $y_s = H/2 - s y/z$, keeping $z$ as depth."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:30
msgid "**Culling and shading.** For every face that faces the eye it computes the Lambert term, attenuates it by the fraction of the face that the shadow map says is lit, and emits two `DrawTriangle`s with that intensity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:31
msgid "**Backend.** A backend turns the `DrawList` into output:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:32
msgid "`backend/tui` squeezes $y$ by `terminal_y_scale`, picks a ramp character per triangle and rasterizes with a depth buffer into a `FrameBuffer`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:33
msgid "`backend/canvas` rasterizes into the frontend `LumaBuffer` and paints runs of equal shade on a canvas;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:34
msgid "`backend/gsap` sorts the triangles far to near and writes SVG polygons."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:35
msgid "**Presentation.** A demo prints the frame, paints it in the browser, or stores it in a `.tui3d` sequence."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:37
msgid "Why the boundary is the draw list"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:39
msgid "All three backends need the same thing: where each triangle is on the screen, how far away it is, and how bright it is. Everything before that point is device-independent geometry and is computed once in the frontend; everything after it is a device decision. The [frontend design](design/frontend.md) discusses the alternatives."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:41
msgid "Occlusion is the one decision that differs between backends. The TUI and Canvas backends use the same depth-buffer rule and are exact. SVG has no depth buffer, so the GSAP backend uses painter's ordering, which is exact for single convex objects and approximate otherwise ([GSAP design](design/backend/gsap.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:43
msgid "Conventions that cross packages"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:45
msgid "**Coordinates.** World and camera space follow the left-handed convention of Direct3D: with the default camera, $+x$ is right, $+y$ up and $+z$ away from the viewer. Screen $y$ grows downwards. Faces are meant to be wound so that $(b - a) \\times (c - a)$ points outwards; `cylinder_mesh`, `cone_mesh` and `triangular_pyramid_mesh` currently wind them inwards (see the [core API](api/core.md#cube_mesh-sphere_mesh-cylinder_mesh-cone_mesh-triangular_pyramid_mesh-torus_mesh))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:46
msgid "**Light direction.** A `Light` stores the unit vector from the scene towards the light."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:47
msgid "**Depth.** Depth is camera-space $z$ everywhere after projection; smaller is nearer. Empty pixels have depth $10^{30}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:48
msgid "**Tolerance.** `@core.DEPTH_EPSILON` = $10^{-9}$ decides degenerate normals, homogeneous division, zero-area triangles and depth-test ties in every package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:49
msgid "**Lenient constructors.** Constructors replace invalid sizes and counts by defaults instead of failing; nothing in the library returns `Result` or raises."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:50
msgid "**Read-only records.** Public structs are `pub struct`: callers can read every field but build values only through constructors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:52
msgid "Not in this repository"
msgstr ""

#: manual/architecture.md:54
msgid "There is no clipping against a near plane, no scene graph, no materials or textures, no smooth shading, no asset loading, no physics and no spatial index. The [repository conventions](conventions.md) ask documentation not to describe such features until they exist."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:1
msgid "Repository conventions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:3
msgid "These rules add to the Luna-Flow documentation standard for `geometry3d`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:5
msgid "Scope"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:7
msgid "Document the version on the current branch, as described by each package's `pkg.generated.mbti`. The JavaScript-only packages (`backend/canvas`, `backend/gsap`, `demo_canvas`, `demo_gsap`) have no committed interface file; their public surface is the one `moon info --target js` reports."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:8
msgid "Do not document speculative engine features such as scene graphs, materials, textures, physics, BVH, or asset loaders unless they are implemented."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:10
msgid "Package boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:12
msgid "Keep geometry core documentation free of terminal, ANSI, and TUI concerns."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:13
msgid "Keep terminal y-scale, background patterns, and character rasterization under `backend/tui` or `demo`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:14
msgid "Keep browser DOM, Canvas, scanline, and JS-target details under `backend/canvas`, `backend/gsap`, `demo_canvas` or `demo_gsap`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:15
msgid "When behavior crosses package boundaries, update the API, design, and tutorial pages of every affected package together."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:17
msgid "Executables"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:19
msgid "The demo packages export no MoonBit items. Their API page documents the command line (`demo`) or the HTML page contract (`demo_canvas`, `demo_gsap`) instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:21
msgid "Examples"
msgstr ""

#: manual/conventions.md:23
msgid "Every `moonbit` block that is not marked `nocheck` must compile against the current code, and the outputs shown in `inspect` snapshots and `text` blocks must be the real outputs. Check them by copying the blocks into a scratch module that depends on this repository and running `moon test` (with `--target js` for the browser pages)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:1
msgid "backend/canvas design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:3
#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:3
#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:3
#: manual/design/core.md:3
#: manual/design/demo.md:3
#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:3
#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:3
#: manual/design/frontend.md:3
#: manual/design/view.md:3
msgid "Design goal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:5
msgid "The Canvas backend shows the same `DrawList` as the terminal backend in a browser, at pixel resolution and in colour, with exact occlusion. It should reuse the frontend's software depth buffer instead of growing a second rasterizer, and keep the number of DOM calls per frame small, because each call from MoonBit into the Canvas API crosses the JavaScript boundary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:7
#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:7
#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:7
#: manual/design/core.md:7
#: manual/design/demo.md:7
#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:7
#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:7
#: manual/design/frontend.md:7
#: manual/design/view.md:7
msgid "Constraints"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:9
msgid "The package runs only on the `js` target and reaches the Canvas 2D API through `moonbit-community/rabbita/dom`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:10
msgid "Every call into the DOM crosses the JavaScript boundary, so the number of calls per frame matters more than the arithmetic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:12
#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:13
#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:12
#: manual/design/core.md:13
#: manual/design/demo.md:12
#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:12
#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:12
#: manual/design/frontend.md:13
#: manual/design/view.md:13
msgid "Mathematical background"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:14
msgid "From intensity to colour"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:16
msgid "Each covered pixel has an intensity $I$ from the depth-tested `LumaBuffer`. The backend quantizes it to one of $L$ shade levels and scales the foreground colour $\\mathbf{c} = (r, g, b)$ by the level:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:23
msgid "As for the TUI ramp, the quantization error of the intensity is at most $1/(2(L - 1))$; with the default $L = 256$ that is below the resolution of an 8-bit channel, so the result is visually continuous. The scaling is linear in the stored channel values. Displays apply the sRGB transfer curve, under which the emitted luminance grows roughly like the stored value to the power 2.2, so mid intensities look darker than a physically linear rendering would. This matches the look of the TUI ramp and is not corrected."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:25
msgid "The background is a separate colour, not shade 0. A covered pixel with $I = 0$ (a face turned away from the light) is painted pure black and stays distinguishable from the background; the `LumaBuffer` depth, not the value, decides what is covered (depth below `LUMA_FAR_DEPTH / 2`)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:27
msgid "Run-length painting"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:29
msgid "Painting pixel by pixel would cost one `fillRect` per pixel. The backend instead scans each row and groups maximal runs of consecutive covered pixels with the same shade $q$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:35
msgid "Painting each run as one rectangle of height 1 produces exactly the same image as painting every covered pixel. The runs are disjoint, their union is the set of covered pixels of the row, and every pixel of a run receives the colour $\\mathbf{c}_q$ of its own shade. The number of rectangles is the number of runs, at most the number of covered pixels and usually far smaller, because a flat-shaded face has one intensity and spans whole runs. The fill style is set only when the shade differs from the previous run's, so a large face of one shade costs one style change per row at most."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:37
msgid "Occlusion"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:39
msgid "Visibility comes from the frontend's depth buffer, whose invariant is derived in the [frontend design](../frontend.md#the-depth-buffer): every pixel shows a covering triangle within `DEPTH_EPSILON` of the nearest one, independent of draw order except among such near-ties. The Canvas output is therefore exact per pixel, unlike the painter's ordering of the [GSAP SVG backend](gsap.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:41
#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:46
#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:75
#: manual/design/core.md:146
#: manual/design/demo.md:55
#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:28
#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:20
#: manual/design/frontend.md:142
#: manual/design/view.md:157
msgid "Design decisions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:43
msgid "Reuse the frontend rasterizer"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:45
msgid "The Canvas 2D API can fill polygons itself, but it has no depth buffer, so occlusion would need sorting and would fail for intersecting triangles. Rasterizing into `LumaBuffer` gives exact occlusion and the same perspective-correct depth as the TUI backend, at the cost of doing the per-pixel work in MoonBit. At 640 × 480 this is fast enough for the animated demos."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:47
msgid "One colour, many shades"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:49
msgid "A single foreground colour scaled by intensity keeps the frontend's contract (a scalar intensity per triangle) and reproduces the monochrome look of the terminal version in colour. Materials and per-object colours would require extending `DrawTriangle`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:51
msgid "Runs instead of `ImageData`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:53
msgid "Writing an `ImageData` buffer and calling `putImageData` once would cost one DOM call per frame, but needs a typed-array binding and per-pixel writes on the JavaScript side. Run-length `fillRect` calls use only the stable 2D context API exposed by `rabbita/dom` and are cheap for flat-shaded scenes, whose runs are long."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:55
msgid "Normalize on every call"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:57
msgid "The configuration is normalized again inside every render call. A configuration built by any route (including by code inside this package) cannot make the renderer divide by zero or emit an invalid CSS colour."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:59
#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:64
#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:97
#: manual/design/core.md:175
#: manual/design/demo.md:73
#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:38
#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:30
#: manual/design/frontend.md:164
#: manual/design/view.md:175
msgid "Correctness and invariants"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:61
msgid "Every pixel of the configured area is painted: first with the background, then covered pixels with their shade."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:62
msgid "Painting runs is pixel-for-pixel equal to painting covered pixels individually."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:63
msgid "Each covered pixel shows the nearest triangle (frontend depth-buffer invariant)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:64
msgid "Channels are always within $[0, 255]$ and `shade_levels` within $[2, 256]$ after normalization."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:66
msgid "The cost per frame is the frontend rasterization plus one $O(W H)$ scan for runs, plus one `fillRect` per run."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:68
#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:73
#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:107
#: manual/design/core.md:186
#: manual/design/demo.md:80
#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:43
#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:35
#: manual/design/frontend.md:175
#: manual/design/view.md:184
msgid "Alternatives rejected"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:70
msgid "**WebGL.** It would move rasterization to the GPU, but needs shaders and buffers and would duplicate the pipeline that the frontend already implements in MoonBit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:71
msgid "**Polygon fills sorted by depth.** Fewer calls, but painter's ordering is wrong for intersecting or cyclically overlapping triangles; the SVG backend accepts that trade-off, the Canvas backend does not."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:72
msgid "**Gamma-correct shading.** It would change the look relative to the terminal output, which is the reference picture of the repository."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:74
#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:79
#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:114
#: manual/design/core.md:193
#: manual/design/demo.md:86
#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:48
#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:40
#: manual/design/frontend.md:182
#: manual/design/view.md:190
msgid "Boundaries"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:76
msgid "The Canvas backend does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:78
msgid "run on targets other than `js`, or work without a DOM;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:79
msgid "schedule animation frames, read input, or look up elements (the `demo_canvas` package does);"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:80
msgid "support per-object colours, textures, transparency, anti-aliasing or GPU acceleration;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:81
msgid "let callers change colours or shade levels from outside the package: `CanvasRenderConfig` fields are read-only there, and only `default` and `sized` construct it;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/canvas.md:82
msgid "apply any aspect-ratio correction (canvas pixels are square)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:1
msgid "backend/gsap design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:5
msgid "The GSAP backend shows a `DrawList` as resolution-independent vector graphics and lets a mature animation library, GSAP, own playback: play, pause, reverse, seek, speed and looping. The scene math stays in MoonBit; JavaScript only stores polygons and runs the clock. The backend has no depth buffer, because SVG has none, so it must decide visibility by drawing order. This page states when that order is exact."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:9
msgid "SVG has no depth buffer: occlusion can only come from the order of the elements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:10
msgid "GSAP is loaded by the page, not by MoonBit, and is found at `globalThis.gsap`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:11
msgid "The package runs only on the `js` target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:15
msgid "Painter's algorithm"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:17
msgid "SVG paints its children in document order, each covering what was painted before. If triangles are emitted from far to near, nearer surfaces cover farther ones. The backend uses the mean camera-space depth of each triangle as its sort key,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:23
msgid "sorted in decreasing order, with ties broken by the position in the draw list, so the result is deterministic."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:25
msgid "**When the order is exact.** Two triangles whose screen projections do not overlap can be drawn in any order. For overlapping ones, the order is correct if the nearer triangle is drawn later. A sufficient condition is that their depth ranges are disjoint: if $\\max_i z_i(A) < \\min_i z_i(B)$, then $\\bar z(A) < \\bar z(B)$, so $B$ is drawn first and $A$, the nearer one everywhere, covers it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:27
msgid "**A single convex object is always exact.** Let the mesh bound a convex solid and let the frontend have removed the back faces. A ray from the eye through a pixel meets the boundary of a convex solid in at most two points, entering through a front face and leaving through a back face. So at most one *front* face lies on each ray, and the projections of distinct front faces overlap at most along shared edges. Every drawing order is then correct, and in particular the sorted one. Cubes, spheres, cylinders, cones and the pyramid are convex."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:29
msgid "**Where it fails.** For non-convex meshes (the torus) and scenes of several objects, overlapping triangles can have overlapping depth ranges. Mean depth is then a heuristic, and two configurations defeat any ordering of whole triangles:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:31
msgid "*cyclic overlap*: $A$ covers part of $B$, $B$ part of $C$, and $C$ part of $A$;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:32
msgid "*interpenetration*: two triangles intersect, so each is in front of the other on one side of the intersection line."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:34
msgid "Correct results would need splitting triangles (Newell's algorithm) or a depth buffer. The [Canvas backend](canvas.md) has a depth buffer and is exact in these cases.[^newell]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:36
msgid "M. E. Newell, R. G. Newell and T. L. Sancha, \"A solution to the hidden surface problem\", Proc. ACM National Conference, 1972."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:38
msgid "Shading"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:40
msgid "Fills use the same quantization as the Canvas backend: $q = \\operatorname{round}(\\operatorname{clamp}(I)\\,(L - 1))$ and colour $\\operatorname{round}(\\mathbf{c}\\, q / (L - 1))$, with error at most $1/(2(L - 1))$ in intensity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:42
msgid "Time as the only input"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:44
msgid "`GsapPlayer` tweens a clock object from $0$ to $D$ seconds with a linear ease. For timeline position $\\tau$ (in GSAP's own time, already scaled by the speed factor), the callback receives $t = \\tau$ for $\\tau \\in [0, D]$. The MoonBit side renders the scene as a pure function of $t$: $\\text{frame}(t) = \\text{render}(\\text{scene}(t))$. Seeking, reversing, looping and changing speed therefore need no extra state: whatever $t$ GSAP reports, the picture for that $t$ is drawn. Updates arrive at the browser's refresh rate, not at a fixed frame rate, so the backend never assumes a frame interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:48
msgid "Vector output with painter's ordering"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:50
msgid "The problem: produce crisp, scalable output that a browser can retain and restyle, from triangles. SVG polygons are exactly that, but SVG has no depth buffer. The options were to rasterize into an image (losing the vector benefits), split triangles to obtain an exact order (complex and slow in the general case), or sort whole triangles. Sorting was chosen: it is exact for the convex primitives of the library, close to correct for typical scenes, and cheap at $O(n \\log n)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:52
msgid "Mean depth as the key, stable ties"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:54
msgid "Mean depth is cheap and symmetric in the vertices. Breaking ties by the original index makes the output deterministic, so tests can assert the exact polygon order, and frames of a static scene do not flicker."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:56
msgid "Reusing DOM nodes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:58
msgid "Each call reuses the existing `<polygon>` elements and only adds or removes the difference in count. Creating thousands of elements per frame would dominate the cost and stress the garbage collector; updating two attributes per polygon does not. The markers `data-gsap-svg-background` and `data-gsap-svg-triangles` let the backend find its own nodes, and it replaces the children of an `<svg>` that does not have them yet."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:60
#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:22
msgid "GSAP owns the clock"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:62
msgid "Playback controls are solved problems in GSAP. The backend exposes a minimal, clamped subset (`seek` within the duration, `set_progress` within $[0, 1]$, a positive time scale, repeat $\\ge -1$) so that MoonBit callers cannot drive the timeline into states the demo UI does not expect. GSAP is looked up at `globalThis.gsap` at construction time rather than imported, so the page decides how to load it (CDN, bundler, or a local copy)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:66
msgid "After `render_draw_list`, the triangle group has exactly as many polygons as the draw list has triangles, ordered by decreasing mean depth with stable ties."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:67
msgid "The picture is exact whenever overlapping triangles have disjoint depth ranges, in particular for any single convex mesh after back-face culling."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:68
msgid "Fill colours are valid `rgb(r, g, b)` strings with channels in $[0, 255]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:69
msgid "`GsapPlayer` keeps $\\text{seek} \\in [0, D]$, $\\text{progress} \\in [0, 1]$, time scale $> 0$ and repeat $\\ge -1$ for values passed through its methods."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:71
msgid "Cost per frame: the frontend pipeline, an $O(n \\log n)$ sort of $n$ triangles, and $O(n)$ attribute updates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:75
msgid "**Per-pixel depth in SVG** (for example one polygon per pixel run) gives up everything SVG is good at."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:76
msgid "**BSP trees or Newell's algorithm.** Both give exact orders, but split triangles and change the polygon count from frame to frame; for demo scenes the visible errors of mean-depth sorting are rare and short-lived."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:77
msgid "**Driving `requestAnimationFrame` from MoonBit.** It would reimplement play, pause, reverse and seek, which GSAP already provides."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:81
msgid "The GSAP backend does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:83
msgid "run on targets other than `js`, or work without a DOM and a global `gsap`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:84
msgid "resolve cyclic overlaps or intersecting triangles;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:85
msgid "add strokes to hide the hairline anti-aliasing seams that can appear between adjacent polygons;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:86
msgid "build the player UI, load GSAP, or choose what to render at a given time (the `demo_gsap` package does);"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/gsap.md:87
msgid "let callers change colours or shade levels from outside the package (the configuration fields are read-only there)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:1
msgid "backend/tui design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:5
msgid "The TUI backend shows the frontend's triangles in a terminal, using only characters. A terminal cell is a coarse, non-square pixel with no colour in this backend, so the package has three jobs: correct the cell shape so that a cube looks like a cube, map a continuous intensity onto a handful of characters, and resolve occlusion per cell. It should produce plain strings that tests, files and video tools can consume, and stay free of ANSI escape codes and terminal I/O, which belong to the demo."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:9
msgid "A terminal cell is a coarse pixel about twice as tall as it is wide, with one character and, in this backend, no colour."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:10
msgid "The output must be plain strings that tests, files and the video exporter can consume on every target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:14
msgid "Non-square cells"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:16
msgid "The frontend projects into a grid of square units: one unit of $x$ and one unit of $y$ have the same physical length. A typical terminal cell is about twice as tall as it is wide. If the projection's $y$ were used directly as a row index, every shape would appear stretched vertically by the cell aspect ratio $\\kappa = h_{\\text{cell}} / w_{\\text{cell}} \\approx 2$. Measuring physical lengths in cell widths, a vertical segment of $\\Delta y$ units must cover $\\Delta y / \\kappa$ rows. `apply_terminal_y_scale` applies this scale about the middle row so that the image stays centred:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:22
msgid "$x$ and the depth are unchanged. The map is affine in screen coordinates, so it preserves barycentric coordinates and hence the perspective-correct depth rule of the [view design](../view.md): interpolating $1/z$ after the squeeze gives the same depth as before it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:24
msgid "Field of view in a terminal"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:26
msgid "With a `ScientificCamera`, the projection scale $s = H f / h$ makes the vertical angle of view span $H$ units, which the squeeze turns into $H/2$ rows. The image is undistorted, but the vertical field of view occupies the middle half of the rows. Equivalently, the full height of the terminal shows a vertical angle of"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:32
msgid "instead of $2 \\arctan\\big(h / (2f)\\big)$. The demos choose their focal lengths with this in mind. The video exporter in `tools/` renders cells with the same $1 : 2$ aspect ratio, so the two transforms cancel physically."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:34
msgid "Shade quantization"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:36
msgid "A ramp of $n$ characters $c_0 \\dots c_{n-1}$, ordered by how much ink they put in a cell, represents intensities by rounding to the nearest of $n$ equally spaced levels:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:43
msgid "The default ramp `\" .:-=+*#%@\"` has $n = 10$, so the quantization error is at most $1/18 \\approx 0.056$. Intensity $0$ maps to the first character, a space, so an unlit face is still drawn and still hides what is behind it. Intensities slightly above 1 from floating-point rounding are clamped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:45
msgid "Rasterization and depth"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:47
msgid "`draw_triangle_z` uses the edge-function rule and the depth invariant derived in the [frontend design](../frontend.md): a cell is covered when its centre $(x + \\tfrac12, y + \\tfrac12)$ lies in the closed triangle, the depth there is $\\big(\\sum \\lambda_i / z_i\\big)^{-1}$, and a write happens only if it is nearer than the stored depth by more than `DEPTH_EPSILON`. After all triangles are drawn, each cell shows the character of a covering triangle whose depth is within `DEPTH_EPSILON` of the nearest one; only the choice among such near-ties depends on the draw order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:49
msgid "Two rendering paths"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:51
msgid "The direct path (`render_draw_list`) chooses a character per triangle and rasterizes characters. The luma path (`draw_list_to_tui_luma` followed by `render_luma_buffer`) rasterizes intensities into a frontend `LumaBuffer` first and quantizes per cell afterwards. For a single frame both give the same characters on lit surfaces. They differ in two ways:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:53
msgid "The luma path quantizes *after* any processing. Averaging $N$ exposures before quantizing yields intermediate shades that averaging characters could not."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:54
msgid "The luma path draws only cells whose value is above $0$, so unlit faces show the background pattern instead of a space."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:56
msgid "File formats"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:58
msgid "Both formats are line-oriented text:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:73
msgid ""
"The decoders split on `'\\n"
"'`, drop `'\\r'`, read the digits after `=` on the header lines (falling back to the defaults), and take the $H$ lines that follow each marker. Round trip: if every frame's content consists of exactly $H$ lines, each terminated by `'\\n"
"'`, containing no `'\\r'`, then `decode(encode(s))` has the same size, frame rate and frame contents as `s`. The encoder writes the header, then each marker followed by those $H$ lines verbatim, and the decoder reads back exactly the $H$ lines after each marker and re-terminates them. The `frames=` count is informational; the decoder counts markers instead, which lets `--record-stdout` stream frames without knowing their number in advance."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:77
msgid "Aspect correction in the backend"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:79
msgid "Non-square cells are a property of the output device, not of the scene or the camera, so the correction lives here and nowhere else. `core`, `view` and `frontend` keep square units, and the Canvas and SVG backends, whose pixels are square, need no correction. The factor is a configuration field so that terminals with other cell shapes can be supported by the package."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:81
msgid "One character per triangle"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:83
msgid "Each triangle has one flat intensity, so the direct path picks its character once and rasterizes characters. This is the cheapest correct choice for flat shading. The luma path exists for the cases that need arithmetic on intensities before quantization."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:85
msgid "Patterns as functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:87
msgid "A background is a function of the cell position and the buffer size, so dots, checkerboards, borders or gradients are all one-liners and need no stored image. `dotted_background` is the default because it shows the extent of the frame in a terminal, where trailing spaces are invisible."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:89
msgid "Strings, not terminal I/O"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:91
msgid "The package returns `String` values and never prints. Clearing the screen, timing, reading `COLUMNS`/`LINES` and writing files are the demo's business, which keeps the backend usable in tests (behavioural assertions instead of brittle full-frame snapshots) and on every MoonBit target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:93
msgid "Lenient, plain-text file formats"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:95
msgid "The formats are readable in a text editor, diffable, and trivially parsed by the Python video exporter. The decoders never fail; they fall back to defaults, because their only consumer, the demo player, prefers showing something to aborting."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:99
msgid "`FrameBuffer::to_string` has exactly `height * (width + 1)` characters."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:100
msgid "`shade_char` returns a ramp character for every input; its error is at most $1/(2(n - 1))$ on $[0, 1]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:101
msgid "The vertical squeeze preserves perspective-correct depth, so occlusion is the same with and without it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:102
msgid "Each cell shows a covering triangle within `DEPTH_EPSILON` of the nearest covering depth; background cells keep depth `FAR_DEPTH`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:103
msgid "Sequences and images round-trip under the condition stated above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:105
msgid "The cost of drawing a triangle is proportional to the area of its bounding box in cells. The box is not clipped to the buffer, so a huge off-screen triangle (from geometry close to the eye) costs time even though it writes nothing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:109
msgid "**ANSI colour or Unicode block and Braille characters.** They give more resolution or colour, but depend on terminal capabilities and fonts. A pure ASCII ramp works everywhere, including in files and in the video exporter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:110
msgid "**Correcting the aspect ratio in the projection.** A non-uniform projection scale would leak a device property into `view` and into every other backend."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:111
msgid "**Dithering.** Error diffusion would add texture to flat faces; with ten levels, faceted shading is already legible."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:112
msgid "**Binary or compressed file formats.** Text keeps the files inspectable and the tools simple; recordings compress well with general-purpose tools if needed."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:116
msgid "The TUI backend does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:118
msgid "print, clear the screen, read terminal size or environment variables, or measure time;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:119
msgid "emit ANSI escape codes, colour, or Unicode block characters;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:120
msgid "clip triangles to the buffer, anti-alias, or dither;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:121
msgid "validate that file contents match the declared width;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/backend/tui.md:122
msgid "let callers change the ramp, background or scale through `TuiRenderConfig` from outside the package (its fields are read-only); the lower-level functions cover those cases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:1
msgid "core design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:5
msgid "`core` is the part of `geometry3d` that every other package agrees on: what a mesh is, how a point moves under a transform, which side of a face is the outside, and how bright a face is under a light. It must be small enough to read in one sitting, exact about its conventions, and free of anything that belongs to a camera, a screen or an output device. It is also a demonstration that the dense types of `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra` are enough to carry a 3D pipeline."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:9
msgid "Vectors and matrices are the dense `Double` types of `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra`; the package adds no vector type of its own."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:10
msgid "Every other package depends on `core`, so it may not depend on cameras, screens or output devices."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:11
msgid "Meshes must be usable by three rasterizers and by back-face culling, so the face type has to carry an orientation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:15
msgid "Homogeneous coordinates"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:17
msgid "An affine map $x \\mapsto A x + t$ of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ is not linear, so it has no 3×3 matrix. Embedding $\\mathbb{R}^3$ in $\\mathbb{R}^4$ fixes this. A point $p$ is written $(p, 1)$ and a direction $d$ is written $(d, 0)$, and the affine map becomes the block matrix"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:25
msgid "The last coordinate records what kind of object a vector is. A direction is the difference of two points, $(p, 1) - (q, 1) = (p - q, 0)$, and the computation above shows that it is moved by $A$ alone: translating both points does not change their difference. This is exactly the split between `Transform3::apply_point` (which appends $w = 1$) and `Transform3::apply_direction` (which appends $w = 0$)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:27
msgid ""
"A general 4×4 matrix also has a last row $(h^\\mathsf{T}, k) \\n"
"e (0, 0, 0, 1)$. It then sends $(p, 1)$ to $(x', y', z', w')$ with $w' = h \\cdot p + k$, and the point it represents is found by the *homogeneous division* $(x'/w', y'/w', z'/w')$. Because $(x', y', z', w')$ and $\\lambda (x', y', z', w')$ give the same point for every $\\lambda \\n"
"e 0$, these matrices act on projective space; they include the perspective projection that `view` uses. `apply_point` always divides, so it handles both cases, and skips the division when $|w'| \\le \\varepsilon$ = `DEPTH_EPSILON`, that is, for points sent to (or near) the plane at infinity."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:29
msgid "Composition"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:31
msgid "For two transforms $M_1$ (applied first) and $M_2$ (applied second) the composite acts on a column vector as $M_2 (M_1 v) = (M_2 M_1) v$ by associativity of the matrix product. `Transform3::compose` stores $M_2 M_1$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:37
msgid "The method reads in application order while the matrix product reads right to left. Two consequences follow from the algebra. Composition is associative, so a chain can be grouped freely. It is not commutative: with $T$ a translation by $t$ and $R$ a rotation,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:45
msgid "unless $R t = t$. The usual model transform therefore scales, then rotates, then translates: `s.compose(r).compose(t)`, matrix $T R S$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:47
msgid "Elementary rotations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:49
msgid "A linear map is determined by the images of the basis vectors, which form the columns of its matrix. Rotating the $xy$-plane by $\\theta$ about the $z$ axis sends"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:55
msgid "which gives the matrix $R_z(\\theta)$ of `rotation_z`. The rotations about $x$ and $y$ follow by cycling the axes $x \\to y \\to z \\to x$: about $x$ the pair $(y, z)$ plays the role of $(x, y)$, and about $y$ the pair $(z, x)$ does. The second substitution is why the minus sign of $R_y$ sits below the diagonal:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:61
msgid "Each $R$ is orthogonal with determinant $+1$: its columns are orthonormal, and $\\det R_z(\\theta) = \\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta = 1$. Hence $R^{-1} = R^\\mathsf{T} = R(-\\theta)$, and rotations preserve lengths, angles and the orientation of a frame (they map a right-handed triple to a right-handed one)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:63
msgid "Euler angles"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:65
msgid "`rotation_matrix(α, β, γ)` composes the elementary rotations as $R = R_z(\\gamma) R_y(\\beta) R_x(\\alpha)$. Writing $c_\\bullet = \\cos$ and $s_\\bullet = \\sin$ of the corresponding angle and multiplying out,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:78
msgid "Applied to a column vector, the rotation about $x$ acts first, then $y$, then $z$, each about the fixed world axes (extrinsic $x$-$y$-$z$, equivalently intrinsic $z$-$y'$-$x''$). A product of rotations is a rotation, so by Euler's rotation theorem $R$ is a single rotation by some angle $\\theta$ about some axis, with $\\operatorname{tr} R = 1 + 2\\cos\\theta$.[^euler]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:80
msgid "The trace is invariant under change of basis, and in a basis whose third vector is the axis the matrix is $R_z(\\theta)$, whose trace is $1 + 2\\cos\\theta$. `geometry3d` does not extract the axis and angle; the identity is useful for checking a composed rotation in a test."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:82
msgid "Euler angles have a singularity. At $\\beta = \\pi/2$ the matrix becomes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:88
msgid "which depends on $\\alpha - \\gamma$ only: changing $\\alpha$ and $\\gamma$ by the same amount gives the same rotation. One degree of freedom is lost (gimbal lock), and near $\\beta = \\pm\\pi/2$ small changes of orientation need large changes of the angles. The demos animate the three angles with independent constant rates, where this does not matter."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:90
msgid "Faces, normals and orientation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:92
msgid "A face $(a, b, c, d)$ lies in a plane when its four vertices do. Its normal is computed from the first three:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:98
msgid "The cross product is antisymmetric, so swapping two vertices reverses $n$; the vertex order *is* the orientation. The convention is that $\\hat n$ points out of the solid; `cube_mesh`, `sphere_mesh` and `torus_mesh` follow it, and `cylinder_mesh`, `cone_mesh` and `triangular_pyramid_mesh` violate it (see below). For the cube face $(0, 3, 2, 1)$ at $z = -s$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:107
msgid "which points to $-z$, away from the cube. For the torus $P(u, v) = ((R + r\\cos v)\\cos u,\\ r \\sin v,\\ (R + r\\cos v)\\sin u)$ the face at $(u, v)$ uses the vertices $P(u, v)$, $P(u, v + \\Delta v)$, $P(u + \\Delta u, v + \\Delta v)$. To first order the two edges are $P_v \\Delta v$ and $P_v \\Delta v + P_u \\Delta u$, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:113
msgid "and at $u = v = 0$, $P_v = (0, r, 0)$ and $P_u = (0, 0, R + r)$ give $P_v \\times P_u = (r (R + r), 0, 0)$: the normal points away from the axis, out of the tube. A test in the repository checks this so that back-face culling cannot regress to showing the inner wall."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:115
msgid "The same computation shows that the other three generators are wound inwards. The first side face of `cylinder_mesh` is $(b_0, b_1, t_1, t_0)$ with $b_k = (r\\cos\\theta_k, -h, r\\sin\\theta_k)$ and $t_k$ the same point at height $+h$. At $\\theta_0 = 0$, to first order in $\\Delta = \\theta_1$,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:122
msgid "which points towards the axis. The bottom cap $(\\text{centre}, b_1, b_0)$ gives $(r\\cos\\Delta, 0, r\\sin\\Delta) \\times (r, 0, 0) = (0, r^2\\sin\\Delta, 0)$, upwards into the solid, and the cone sides and the pyramid faces are wound the same way. Back-face culling therefore keeps the far, inner side of these three solids. This is a defect of the generators; the [core API](../api/core.md#cube_mesh-sphere_mesh-cylinder_mesh-cone_mesh-triangular_pyramid_mesh-torus_mesh) shows a workaround."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:124
msgid "Every quad the generators emit is planar, so its normal is well defined. For the sphere and the torus, the face between the parameters $u, u'$ is symmetric under the reflection in the plane through the $y$ axis at angle $(u + u')/2$, which swaps $P(u, v) \\leftrightarrow P(u', v)$ and $P(u, v') \\leftrightarrow P(u', v')$. The segments $P(u, v)P(u', v)$ and $P(u, v')P(u', v')$ are both perpendicular to that mirror plane, hence parallel, and two parallel segments span a plane. Cube, cylinder sides and degenerate quads are planar by construction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:126
msgid "Back-face visibility"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:128
msgid "A point $e$ sees the front side of a planar face exactly when it lies in the open half-space the normal points into:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:134
msgid "The sign does not depend on the choice of $q$: for two points $q, q'$ of the plane, $\\hat n \\cdot (q - q') = 0$, so $\\hat n \\cdot (e - q) = \\hat n \\cdot (e - q')$. `face_is_visible` takes $q$ = `face_center`. For a closed solid, a face whose back is turned to the eye is hidden by the front faces of the same solid, so culling it never removes visible surface; it roughly halves the work of the rasterizers. It does not resolve occlusion between different front faces; that is the job of the depth buffer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:136
msgid "Lambert shading"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:138
msgid "A small surface patch of area $A$ with unit normal $\\hat n$, lit by parallel rays travelling against the unit direction $\\ell$, intercepts the flux crossing an area $A \\cos\\theta$ perpendicular to the rays, where $\\cos\\theta = \\hat n \\cdot \\ell$. A perfectly diffuse (Lambertian) surface reflects light equally in all directions, so its apparent brightness is proportional to $\\cos\\theta$, and to zero when the light is behind the surface:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:144
msgid "`face_intensity` returns exactly this, which is why `light` must be a unit vector pointing towards the light. One value per face gives flat (faceted) shading."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:148
msgid "Quads as the canonical topology"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:150
msgid "The problem: meshes need one face type that every generator, the culling test and the rasterizers understand. The options were triangles only, quads only, or general polygons. Quads were chosen because the parametric surfaces of the generators (sphere, cylinder, torus) are grids in $(u, v)$, whose cells are quads; one quad has one normal and one shading value, which halves the face count of flat shading. Triangles are embedded as degenerate quads with $d = a$, so a pyramid, a cone side or a sphere cap fits the same type. `triangulate_quad` turns a quad into two triangles only where a rasterizer needs them, and the zero-area second triangle of a degenerate quad is dropped by the area test."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:152
msgid "Points and directions as separate operations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:154
msgid "`Transform3` stores one 4×4 matrix and exposes `apply_point` and `apply_direction` instead of asking callers to build $w$ themselves. This keeps the homogeneous convention inside the package and makes the rule \"directions ignore translations\" impossible to get wrong at a call site."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:156
msgid "Normals recomputed, not transformed"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:158
msgid "Normals do not transform like directions under a non-uniform scale: the correct matrix for normals is $(A^{-1})^\\mathsf{T}$, because the tangent $t$ of a surface satisfies $n \\cdot t = 0$, and after the map $t' = A t$ the vector $n' = A^{-\\mathsf{T}} n$ keeps $n' \\cdot t' = n^\\mathsf{T} A^{-1} A t = 0$. Instead of storing normals and transforming them with this matrix, `core` recomputes $\\hat n$ from transformed vertices every time (`face_normal` after `apply_mesh`). The two agree because of the cofactor identity"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:165
msgid "which follows from $w \\cdot \\big((Au) \\times (Av)\\big) = \\det[\\,w \\mid Au \\mid Av\\,] = \\det A \\cdot \\det[\\,A^{-1} w \\mid u \\mid v\\,] = \\det A \\cdot (A^{-1} w) \\cdot (u \\times v)$ for every $w$. Applied to the two edge vectors of a face, the recomputed normal is $\\det A$ times the transformed one. The cost is one cross product per face; the benefit is that normals are always correct for any affine map with $\\det A > 0$, including non-uniform scales. A map with $\\det A < 0$ (a reflection) multiplies every normal by a negative number, so it reverses the winding and turns every face inside out."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:167
msgid "Euler angles instead of axis-angle or quaternions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:169
msgid "The demos only need to spin objects with independent rates about three axes, which is exactly what Euler angles express, and the matrices are three lines each. Axis-angle construction (Rodrigues' formula) and quaternion composition are not implemented; `Luna-Flow/quaternion` is the place for them. Any rotation matrix produced elsewhere can be passed in with `Transform3::from_matrix`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:171
msgid "A shared tolerance"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:173
msgid "`DEPTH_EPSILON` = $10^{-9}$ is used for every \"is this zero?\" decision in the pipeline: normalization, homogeneous division, triangle area, and the strict depth tests `depth + ε < stored`. One constant makes the behaviour at the degenerate boundary consistent across packages. It is an absolute tolerance, so it assumes scene coordinates of order $1$ to $10^3$; the demos use units of about one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:177
msgid "`apply_point` on an affine transform is exact up to floating-point rounding: $w' = 1$, so no division error is introduced."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:178
msgid "`compose` satisfies $\\texttt{t1.compose(t2).apply\\_point}(p) = \\texttt{t2.apply\\_point}(\\texttt{t1.apply\\_point}(p))$ for affine transforms, and up to the homogeneous scale for projective ones. A test checks the order with a translation followed by a scale."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:179
msgid "Rotation matrices are orthogonal with determinant $1$; products of them are again rotations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:180
msgid "Generated meshes are closed, centred on the origin, with planar faces. `face_is_visible` and `face_intensity` rely on outward normals, which `cube_mesh`, `sphere_mesh` and `torus_mesh` have and `cylinder_mesh`, `cone_mesh` and `triangular_pyramid_mesh` do not."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:181
msgid "`normalize_vec` and `face_normal` never divide by a number smaller than `DEPTH_EPSILON`; degenerate inputs yield the zero vector, which makes the face invisible and unlit."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:182
msgid "Every operation allocates a new vector or mesh; no function mutates its arguments. `apply_mesh` shares the face array of its input."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:184
msgid "Each function is constant time except the generators and `apply_mesh`, which are linear in the number of vertices and faces."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:188
msgid "**A dedicated small-vector type** (`Vec3` struct with `x`, `y`, `z` fields) would be faster and type-safe about dimensions, but would duplicate `linear-algebra`. The point of the repository is to build on the Luna-Flow base, so vectors are `@la.Vector[Double]` and dimensions are a convention."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:189
msgid "**Generic scalars** (`Mesh[T]` over any `Field`) were rejected: trigonometry, square roots and the tolerance all assume `Double`, and no backend could use another type."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:190
msgid "**Storing normals in the mesh** was rejected for the reason given above: recomputing them is cheap and always correct after a transform."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:191
msgid "**Triangle meshes as the primary type** would make every flat-shaded grid face two faces with two identical normals."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:195
msgid "`core` does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:197
msgid "know about cameras, projections, viewports, terminals, colours or the DOM;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:198
msgid "let code outside the package build arbitrary meshes: the fields of `Mesh` and `QuadFace` are read-only outside it, so the generators are the only source of meshes;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:199
msgid "load or save meshes, or provide scene graphs, materials, textures, physics or spatial indices;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:200
msgid "compute smooth (per-vertex) normals, clip geometry, or test intersections;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/core.md:201
msgid "provide inverse transforms, axis-angle or quaternion rotations."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:1
msgid "demo design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:5
msgid "The terminal demo shows that the library packages compose into a working renderer with very little glue: a scene, a camera, `build_draw_list`, and a backend. It also exercises the parts of the library that a test cannot judge by eye (perspective, lighting, shadows, the dolly zoom, exposure effects) and produces recordings that can be turned into video. Everything that touches the operating system lives here: argument parsing, environment variables, the clock, the terminal and files."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:9
msgid "The demo is the only package allowed to touch the clock, environment variables, the terminal and files."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:10
msgid "The portable standard library has no sleep primitive and no terminal-size query."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:14
msgid "The dolly zoom"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:16
msgid "With the scientific camera, an object of height $Y$ at distance $d$ along the view axis appears $s\\,Y/d$ rows tall (before the terminal squeeze), with $s = H f / h$ (see the [view design](view.md)). Keeping the subject's size constant while the camera moves requires"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:24
msgid "The scenes use these values:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:26
msgid "Scene"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:26
msgid "Distance $d$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:26
msgid "Base $(d_0, f_0)$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:26
msgid "Focal range"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:28
msgid "$5.7 + 2.3 \\sin(0.035\\,k)$ for frame $k$, in $[3.4, 8]$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:28
msgid "13.6–32 mm"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:29
msgid "$3.2 + 66.365 \\cdot \\tfrac12\\big(1 + \\sin(0.028\\,k)\\big)$, in $[3.2, 69.565]$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:29
msgid "23–500 mm"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:31
msgid "The far distance of the dolly scene, $69.565\\ldots = 3.2 \\cdot 500 / 23$, is chosen so that the focal length peaks at exactly 500 mm. An object at distance $d + \\Delta$ from the camera, behind the subject, appears scaled by"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:37
msgid "relative to the base view. The factor increases with $d$ towards $(d_0 + \\Delta)/d_0$ as $d \\to \\infty$. The wall of discs stands $\\Delta = 5$ units behind the cube, so the limit is $8.2/3.2 \\approx 2.56$, and at the far end of the dolly, $d = 3.2 \\cdot 500/23$, the wall is"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:43
msgid "times its base size while the cube stays fixed. The camera on the long end approaches an orthographic projection, which is why the background flattens. Tests check that the subject's projected size ratio stays constant across frames."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:47
msgid "The exposure modes approximate the time integral of the image over the shutter interval (see the [frontend design](frontend.md)) with $N = 12$ samples:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:53
msgid "where $\\delta$ is one animation step (one frame for the dolly scenes, one rotation increment for the mesh scenes). With `--flow-exposure` each sample $L_j$ is first warped onto the current frame by the block-matching flow $d_j = \\text{flow}(L_j, L_{\\text{current}})$ with search radius 4 and patch radius 1. `ExposureSettings::auto` is consulted, but it always returns one sample, so the demo's own constant of 12 decides the count."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:57
msgid "All system access in one package"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:59
msgid "Reading `COLUMNS` and `LINES`, timing frames, clearing the screen with ANSI escapes, and file I/O through `moonbitlang/x/fs` are the only non-portable operations of the repository, and they all live in `src/demo/main.mbt`. The library packages stay pure and target-independent. The demo loses one row to the shell prompt so that printing a full frame does not scroll."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:61
msgid "A small, forgiving command line"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:63
msgid "Options are plain flags matched exactly, with a fixed precedence and silent fallbacks. This keeps the parser to a few lines and makes the demo hard to crash from the command line, at the cost of not reporting typos. Numeric options keep only their digits for the same reason."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:65
msgid "Busy-wait frame pacing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:67
msgid "The live loops compare `@env.now()` against the last frame time and spin until 33 ms have passed. This needs no sleep primitive, which the portable standard library lacks, and gives stable pacing. The cost is one busy CPU core while the demo runs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:69
msgid "Streaming recordings"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:71
msgid "`--record` builds a `TuiSequence` in memory and encodes it at the end, which is simple and exercises the library encoder. `--record-stdout` prints the same format frame by frame, so recordings of thousands of large frames need memory for only one frame; the format's `frames=` header can be written first because the frame count is known from the timeline."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:75
msgid "The subject of the dolly scenes keeps its projected size: $f/d$ is constant by construction (tested)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:76
msgid "Every frame has exactly the configured width and height, so recordings decode with the sizes in their header."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:77
msgid "A recording's frame count is `Timeline::frame_count()` = $\\lceil \\mathit{duration} \\cdot \\mathit{fps} \\rceil$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:78
msgid "Exposure weights sum to 1, so an exposure frame stays within the intensity range of its samples."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:82
msgid "**An argument-parsing library** would add a dependency for a dozen flags."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:83
msgid "**Raw terminal mode and incremental redraws** would reduce flicker but need platform-specific terminal control."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:84
msgid "**Sleeping between frames** would need FFI on each target."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:88
msgid "The demo does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:90
msgid "export any MoonBit API, or run on targets other than `native`;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:91
msgid "read input while running, resize with the terminal, or offer configuration files;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:92
msgid "validate options or report unknown ones;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo.md:93
msgid "render in colour or in the browser (see [demo_canvas](demo_canvas.md) and [demo_gsap](demo_gsap.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:1
msgid "demo_canvas design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:5
msgid "`demo_canvas` shows the Canvas backend on a real page with as little browser code as possible: one canvas, one selector, one animation loop. It reuses the scenes of the terminal demo, so the two outputs can be compared directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:9
msgid "The program runs in a browser page it does not create: the elements it needs must be present in `index.html`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:10
msgid "It may only use the public API of the library packages and `rabbita`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:14
msgid "Time from animation frames"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:16
msgid "`requestAnimationFrame` passes a timestamp $\\tau$ in milliseconds since the page started. The torus scene turns by the angle $\\theta = 0.0006\\,\\tau$, so one full turn about $y$ takes $2\\pi / 0.0006 \\approx 10.5$ seconds, independent of the display's refresh rate. The dolly scene is defined per frame of the terminal demo, so the page converts time into a frame number at 30 frames per second,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:22
msgid "and renders frame $k$. On a 60 Hz display each dolly frame is shown twice; the motion runs at the same speed as in the terminal."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:24
msgid "Framing"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:26
msgid "The canvas is 640 × 480 (4:3) and the sensor full-frame (3:2). With the projection scale $s = H f / h$, the vertical angle of view fills the canvas height and the horizontal field is narrower than the lens's horizontal angle, as derived in the [view design](view.md). Canvas pixels are square, so unlike the terminal no aspect correction is applied."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:30
msgid "Rendering every frame from scratch"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:32
msgid "Each callback rebuilds the scene, the draw list and the whole canvas. Nothing is cached, which keeps the program a pure function of the timestamp and the selection, and costs nothing that matters at 640 × 480."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:34
msgid "Selection through a `Ref`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:36
msgid "The `change` handler only writes the selected scene into a `Ref`; the animation loop reads it on the next frame. Rendering happens in one place, and switching scenes needs no coordination with the loop."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:40
msgid "The canvas bitmap is 640 × 480 and matches the projection's viewport."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:41
msgid "The picture at time $\\tau$ depends only on $\\tau$ and the current selection."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:45
msgid "**Separate pages per scene** would duplicate the set-up code."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:46
msgid "**A fixed frame counter** instead of the timestamp would make the speed depend on the refresh rate."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_canvas.md:50
msgid "`demo_canvas` does not export an API, run outside a browser, offer playback controls (see [demo_gsap](demo_gsap.md)), or adapt to the window size."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:1
msgid "demo_gsap design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:5
msgid "`demo_gsap` shows that a `geometry3d` scene can be scrubbed, reversed and replayed like any GSAP animation, because each picture is a pure function of time. It is a complete player page with the smallest possible amount of JavaScript."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:9
msgid "The program runs in a browser page it does not create, and GSAP must be loaded by that page before the program starts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:10
msgid "It may only use the public API of the library packages, `rabbita` and a few small `extern \"js\"` helpers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:14
msgid "Scenes as functions of time"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:16
msgid "GSAP reports the timeline time $t \\in [0, 8]$ seconds and the program draws $\\text{render}(\\text{scene}(t))$. Nothing depends on the previous frame, so seeking to any $t$, playing backwards or at another speed shows exactly the picture for that $t$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:18
msgid "The dolly distance $d(t) = 3.2 + 66.37 \\cdot \\tfrac12\\big(1 + \\sin(\\pi t/2)\\big)$ has period 4 s, so the 8-second loop contains two full cycles and joins seamlessly at $t = 8$. The focal length follows $f = 23\\, d / 3.2$ mm, which keeps the cube's projected size constant as derived in the [demo design](demo.md). The torus angle $1.8\\,t$ advances $14.4$ rad per loop, which is not a whole number of turns ($2\\pi \\cdot 2.29$), so the torus jumps slightly when the loop restarts."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:24
msgid "All playback state (paused, direction, speed, repeat, position) lives in the GSAP timeline, and the controls call `GsapPlayer` methods directly. The MoonBit program keeps only the selected scene. Changing the scene re-renders the current time immediately, so a paused player updates too."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:26
msgid "Thin JavaScript glue"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:28
msgid "Reading `event.target.value`, setting a range input and writing text are done in three `extern \"js\"` functions. They are page-specific and would not be reused, so they are not part of the backend."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:32
msgid "The picture shown for timeline time $t$ is independent of how the playhead reached $t$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:33
msgid "The dolly scene is periodic in the loop; the torus scene is not (see above)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:37
msgid "**Keeping a frame counter in MoonBit** would break seeking and reversing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:38
msgid "**Building the controls from MoonBit** with `rabbita` would add code without changing behaviour; the static HTML is simpler."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/demo_gsap.md:42
msgid "`demo_gsap` does not export an API, work offline as shipped, or resolve the painter's-ordering artefacts of the backend (see the [GSAP design](backend/gsap.md))."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:1
msgid "frontend design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:5
msgid "The frontend is where a 3D scene stops being 3D. It owns every step that is the same for all outputs (model and view transforms, projection, visibility, lighting and shadows) and hands the backends a flat list of screen-space triangles with one brightness each. A backend can then be written in an afternoon: it only decides how a triangle of a given brightness looks on its device. The same boundary lets the TUI, Canvas and SVG backends show the same picture from the same `DrawList`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:9
msgid "The backends have very different output models (a character grid, a pixel canvas, retained SVG nodes), and none of them may see world-space geometry."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:10
msgid "Everything runs on the CPU in MoonBit, at demo scale (a few thousand triangles per frame)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:11
msgid "Rendering must be deterministic and testable: no clock, no randomness, no global state."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:15
msgid "The pipeline"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:17
msgid "For each object with mesh vertices $p$ and model matrix $M$, and a camera with view matrix $V$ and projection $\\pi$ (see the [view design](view.md)), `build_draw_list` computes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:23
msgid "and for each face $F$ with camera-space normal $\\hat n_F$ and centre $c_F$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:30
msgid "with the camera at the origin of camera space, $\\ell_c = \\operatorname{normalize}(V \\ell)$ the light direction in camera space (a direction, so $w = 0$), the ambient factor $\\alpha = 0.35$, and the shadow visibility $v_F \\in [0, 1]$ below. Since $V$ is a rigid motion, $\\hat n_F \\cdot \\ell_c$ equals the world-space Lambert term; computing it in camera space avoids a second set of normals. Each emitted quad becomes the two triangles of `triangulate_quad`, both carrying $I_F$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:32
msgid "Shadow mapping"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:34
msgid "A point is in shadow when some other surface lies between it and the light. For a directional light all rays are parallel to $\\ell$, so the test reduces to comparing distances along $\\ell$ inside a parallel (orthographic) projection.[^williams]"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:36
msgid "Lance Williams, \"Casting curved shadows on curved surfaces\", SIGGRAPH 1978, introduced the depth-map technique."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:38
msgid "**Light camera.** Let $c$ be the centre of the scene's world-space bounding box and $\\rho = \\max(1, \\max_p \\lVert p - c \\rVert)$. The frontend places a `Camera3` at $c + (2\\rho + 1)\\,\\ell$ looking at $c$, with up $= +y$ (or $+z$ when $|\\ell \\cdot y| > 0.99$, to keep the frame non-degenerate). Every vertex is at least $\\rho + 1$ units in front of it. In this camera's space, $x_\\ell$ and $y_\\ell$ are coordinates across the light rays, and $z_\\ell$ is the distance along them away from the light."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:40
msgid "**Grid.** Over the light-space bounding box of all vertices, padded on each side by $\\max(0.05 \\cdot \\text{span}, 0.25)$, the map lays an $N \\times N$ grid with $N = 128$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:47
msgid ""
"**Depth pass.** Every triangle of every object, front- or back-facing, is rasterized into the grid with the rule below, keeping in each texel the smallest $z_\\ell$ (the surface nearest the light). Depth is interpolated linearly with the barycentric coordinates, which is exact here: on a plane $a x + b y + c z = d$ with $c \\n"
"e 0$, $z = (d - a x - b y)/c$ is affine in $(x, y)$, and the grid map is affine too."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:49
msgid "**Lookup.** To test a world point $q$, the frontend moves it towards the light by twice the bias $b$, maps it into the grid and rounds to the nearest texel $(X^\\ast, Y^\\ast)$. Moving by $2b$ along $\\ell$ lowers $z_\\ell$ by exactly $2b$, since the light camera's forward axis is $-\\ell$. The point counts as lit if it falls outside the grid, if the texel is empty, or if"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:57
msgid "so the effective tolerance is $3b$, with $b = \\max(0.005 \\cdot \\text{depth span}, 10^{-4})$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:59
msgid "**Why a bias is needed.** A surface shadowing itself because of the grid's finite resolution is called shadow acne. Let $\\Delta = (x_{\\max} - x_{\\min})/(N - 1)$ be the texel size in light-space units (and likewise in $y$). The depth pass samples texel $(i, j)$ at the grid point $(i + \\tfrac12, j + \\tfrac12)$, like every rasterizer of the repository, but the lookup reads the texel $(\\operatorname{round} X, \\operatorname{round} Y)$. For $X \\in [i - \\tfrac12, i + \\tfrac12)$ the stored sample lies at $i + \\tfrac12$, so it is displaced from the lookup point by"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:65
msgid "texels along each axis: up to a whole texel, and half a texel on average, always towards larger $X$ and $Y$.[^offset] If the surface through $q$ has depth gradient $(z_x, z_y)$ in light space, the stored depth of its texel therefore differs from $z_\\ell(q)$ by at most"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:71
msgid "Reading texel $(\\lfloor X \\rfloor, \\lfloor Y \\rfloor)$, or sampling the depth pass at integer grid points, would make the two grids agree and halve the bound. The code does neither; the bound above describes it as it is."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:73
msgid "A face whose normal makes an angle $\\theta$ with $\\ell$ has $\\lVert (z_x, z_y) \\rVert = \\tan\\theta$, so the error grows without bound at grazing incidence. The tolerance $3b$ removes acne whenever $\\sqrt2\\,\\tan\\theta\\,\\Delta \\le 3b$. Where it does not, $\\theta$ is large and the Lambert factor $\\cos\\theta$ already makes the face darker, but the residual acne is not negligible: the five lookups of a face also land on texels written by neighbouring, steeper faces, and on a single `sphere_mesh(1.5, 12, 24)` under `Light::default`, over 200 orientations, about 15% of the lit visible faces lose some visibility although a convex object cannot shadow itself (about 1% of the faces whose Lambert term exceeds $0.5$). The price of the bias is that a shadow starts slightly late at contact points (\"peter-panning\"), by about $3b$ = 1.5% of the scene's depth span."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:75
msgid "**Face visibility.** The frontend tests the centre and the four vertices of each face and averages:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:81
msgid "A face crossing a shadow boundary gets an intermediate value, which softens the boundary at face resolution instead of drawing a jagged edge across a flat-shaded face. The ambient term then keeps a fully shadowed face at $\\alpha = 35\\%$ of its Lambert brightness, so shape stays readable in shadow. Faces turned away from the light are not looked up; their intensity is $0$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:83
msgid "Rasterization by edge functions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:85
msgid "`LumaBuffer::draw_triangle` (and the TUI and shadow-map rasterizers, which use the same rule) decide coverage with edge functions. For points $a$, $b$, $p$ in the screen plane let"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:91
msgid "an affine function of $p$ that vanishes on the line $ab$ and changes sign across it; $|E(a, b; p)|$ is twice the area of the triangle $abp$. For a triangle $p_0 p_1 p_2$ put"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:98
msgid "Each $w_i$ is affine in $p$ and vanishes at the two vertices other than $p_i$. At $p = p_i$ it equals $A$, because the signed area is invariant under cyclic permutation of the vertices. Hence $w_0 + w_1 + w_2 - A$ is an affine function that vanishes at three non-collinear points, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:106
msgid "are the barycentric coordinates of $p$ ($\\lambda_i(p_j) = \\delta_{ij}$, $\\sum \\lambda_i = 1$). The point lies in the closed triangle exactly when all $\\lambda_i \\ge 0$, that is when all $w_i$ have the sign of $A$ or are zero. The code tests both signs, so the screen winding of a triangle does not matter: culling has already happened in 3D. Pixels are sampled at their centres $(x + \\tfrac12, y + \\tfrac12)$, only inside the triangle's bounding box, and triangles with $|A| \\le$ `DEPTH_EPSILON` are skipped. The depth at a covered pixel is the perspective-correct $\\big(\\sum \\lambda_i / z_i\\big)^{-1}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:108
msgid "The depth buffer"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:110
msgid "`set_if_closer` writes a pixel only when the new depth is smaller than the stored one by more than $\\varepsilon$ = `DEPTH_EPSILON`. Fix a pixel, let $d_1, d_2, \\dots$ be the depths of the triangles that cover it in drawing order, and let $s_k$ be the stored depth after the first $k$ of them ($s_0 = 10^{30}$). Then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:120
msgid "By induction: if $d_{k+1} + \\varepsilon < s_k$, the write happens and $s_{k+1} = d_{k+1} < s_k - \\varepsilon \\le d_i$ for every earlier $i$; otherwise $s_{k+1} = s_k \\le d_{k+1} + \\varepsilon$, and the earlier bounds still hold. So every pixel shows a covering triangle whose depth is within $\\varepsilon$ of the nearest one, and triangles that are separated in depth by more than $\\varepsilon$ are resolved independently of the drawing order, which is why the draw list is not sorted. Among triangles closer than $\\varepsilon$ to each other the winner depends on the order, and it is not always the first drawn or the nearest: for the depths $1$, $1 - 0.9\\varepsilon$, $1 - 1.8\\varepsilon$ drawn in this order the second triangle never writes (it is not $\\varepsilon$ nearer than the first), and the third does."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:122
msgid "Edges are inclusive, without a \"top-left\" tie rule: a pixel centre exactly on an edge shared by two triangles is covered by both. The depth test keeps one of them. Both triangles of a quad have the same intensity, so this is invisible inside a face."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:124
msgid "Exposure and optical flow"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:126
msgid "A camera with shutter time $T$ records $\\int_0^T L(x, t)\\,dt$ at each pixel. The frontend approximates the normalized exposure by a Riemann sum over $N$ rendered samples,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:132
msgid "implemented by `LumaBuffer::add_weighted_sample` with weight $1/N$. Moving edges smear into motion blur."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:134
msgid "The optional flow alignment assumes brightness constancy, $L_{\\text{prev}}(x + d) \\approx L_{\\text{cur}}(x)$, and estimates the integer displacement $d$ per pixel by exhaustive block matching:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:140
msgid "Warping each sample by its flow before accumulating (`align_with_flow`) re-registers moving content onto the current frame, which trades blur for sharper, ghost-reduced edges. The estimate is integer-valued. It suffers from the aperture problem: in a uniform patch every $d$ fits, and the scan order then returns $(-R, -R)$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:144
msgid "The draw list is the boundary"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:146
msgid "The problem: three backends with very different output models (a character grid, a pixel canvas, retained SVG nodes) must show the same scene. Options were to hand them meshes (each backend re-implements projection and lighting), pixels (the SVG backend loses its vector output), or projected, shaded triangles. The last was chosen. A `DrawTriangle` contains exactly what every backend needs: screen coordinates, camera depth for occlusion, and a brightness in $[0, 1]$ that each backend maps to its own palette. Projection, culling, lighting and shadows are computed once, in one place, and tested once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:148
msgid "Flat shading per quad"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:150
msgid "One intensity per face matches the faceted, low-polygon look of the demos and the coarse resolution of a terminal, and makes the draw list small. Smooth shading would need per-vertex normals, which `core` does not have, and an interpolated intensity per pixel, which the SVG backend cannot express."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:152
msgid "Shadows in the frontend"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:154
msgid "Shadows need the world-space geometry of the whole scene, which backends never see, so they belong in the frontend. A shadow map was chosen over ray casting: it costs one rasterization of the scene from the light and one lookup per sample point, reuses the existing rasterizer, and needs no acceleration structure. The resolution is fixed at 128 × 128 because visibility is only sampled five times per face; finer maps would not change flat-shaded output. The bounds are fitted to the scene every frame, so the full resolution is always used."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:156
msgid "A shared scalar buffer"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:158
msgid "`LumaBuffer` sits in the frontend, not in a backend, because three consumers need a scalar image with depth: the Canvas backend, the exposure accumulation and the optical flow. The TUI backend keeps its own character buffer for direct rendering, and uses `LumaBuffer` (through `draw_list_to_tui_luma`) for exposure effects."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:160
msgid "Time as data"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:162
msgid "`Timeline`, `ScalarTrack`, `ExposureSettings` and the flow functions are pure data and functions of buffers; they never call a clock. The demos decide when to render and which times to sample, which keeps the frontend deterministic and testable."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:166
msgid "Emitted triangles face the camera ($\\hat n \\cdot (0 - c) > 0$ in camera space) and have intensity in $[0, 1]$, up to rounding (a fully lit face may come out as $1 + 2^{-52}$), when the light direction is a unit vector; backends clamp it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:167
msgid "The draw list is in scene order, object by object and face by face; two triangles per visible quad."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:168
msgid "`LumaBuffer` holds, per pixel, the value of a covering triangle whose depth is within $\\varepsilon$ of the nearest covering depth, as shown above; which one of several near-ties wins depends on the drawing order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:169
msgid "The shadow lookup never darkens a point that lies outside the map or under an empty texel; visibility is a multiple of $0.2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:170
msgid "`ExposureSettings` always has $0 < \\text{shutter} \\le \\text{frame\\_dt}$ and at least one sample; `auto` yields exactly one sample (the shutter is clamped before the count is taken)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:171
msgid "`Timeline::frame_count` is at least 1; sample times are $k/\\mathit{fps}$; progress is clamped to $[0, 1]$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:173
msgid "Costs per frame: $O(V + F)$ for transforms, culling and shading; rasterization proportional to the covered bounding-box area of each triangle (for the shadow map and for `LumaBuffer`); five shadow lookups per lit visible face; $O(W H (2R+1)^2 (2P+1)^2)$ for optical flow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:177
msgid "**Sorting the draw list by depth.** Correct occlusion comes from the depth buffer in the TUI and Canvas backends; the SVG backend sorts on its own because it has no depth buffer. Sorting in the frontend would impose one strategy on all."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:178
msgid "**Gouraud or Phong shading.** See \"Flat shading per quad\"; it would also make the TUI output noisier, not better."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:179
msgid "**Percentage-closer filtering over neighbouring texels.** Five samples per face already give the intermediate values that flat shading can show."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:180
msgid "**Clamping `ExposureSettings::auto` differently.** Allowing a shutter longer than a frame would let `auto` return several samples, but would make one frame integrate light from the next frame's interval."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:184
msgid "The frontend does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:186
msgid "clip geometry against the view volume or a near plane: all vertices must be in front of the camera;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:187
msgid "support point or spot lights, coloured light, several lights, materials or textures;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:188
msgid "produce characters, colours, pixels on a device, or files;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:189
msgid "sort, batch or cache draw lists between frames;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/frontend.md:190
msgid "estimate sub-pixel or dense variational optical flow."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:1
msgid "view design"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:5
msgid "`view` answers one question: where on the screen does a world-space point appear, and how far away is it? The answer must be exact enough that every backend (terminal cells, canvas pixels, SVG polygons) shows the same picture, simple enough to state in three formulas, and parameterized the way a photographer thinks: a sensor, a lens and a distance, rather than an abstract field-of-view number."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:9
msgid "The projection must give the same picture on terminal cells, canvas pixels and SVG coordinates, so it works in viewport units and leaves the cell shape to the backends."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:10
msgid "There is no clipper anywhere in the pipeline, so every projected point is assumed to lie in front of the camera."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:11
msgid "Depth must stay comparable between triangles drawn by different rasterizers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:15
msgid "The pipeline has three stages, each a map between coordinate systems:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:25
msgid "The camera frame"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:27
msgid "`Camera3` is given by an eye $e$, a target $t$ and an approximate up vector $a$. It derives"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:35
msgid "These three vectors form an orthonormal frame. $r$ is perpendicular to $f$ by construction of the cross product, and $u = f \\times r$ is perpendicular to both; $\\lVert u \\rVert = \\lVert f \\rVert \\lVert r \\rVert \\sin 90° = 1$, so the final normalization in the code changes nothing but rounding. The triple product gives the orientation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:41
msgid "$u$ is the component of $a$ perpendicular to $f$, normalized: by the same identity $f \\times (a \\times f) = a - (a \\cdot f)\\, f$, so \"up\" on the screen is as close to $a$ as the viewing direction allows. If $a \\parallel f$ the cross product vanishes, `normalize_vec` returns zero, and the frame degenerates; this is a precondition of `look_at`, not a checked error."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:43
msgid "The view transform"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:45
msgid "Let $R = [\\,r \\mid u \\mid f\\,]$ be the matrix with the frame as columns. It is orthogonal, so $R^{-1} = R^\\mathsf{T}$. The camera-to-world map sends camera coordinates $c$ to $e + R c$; inverting it gives the world-to-camera map"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:59
msgid "which is exactly the matrix that `Camera3::view_transform` builds. Two checks: $V e = 0$ (the eye goes to the origin) and $V t = (0, 0, \\lVert t - e \\rVert)$ (the target lies straight ahead on the positive $z$ axis). Because $V$ is a rigid motion ($\\det R^\\mathsf{T} = +1$), it preserves lengths, angles and dot products. In particular the Lambert term $\\hat n \\cdot \\ell$ has the same value in world and camera space, which is why the frontend may compute lighting after the view transform."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:61
msgid "Handedness"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:63
msgid "The screen shows $r$ to the right and $u$ upwards, and $f = r \\times u$ points into the screen. In a right-handed reading of the axes, right $\\times$ up points *towards* the viewer; here it points away. The convention is therefore the left-handed one of Direct3D: with `Camera3::default`, world $+x$ appears on the right, $+y$ up, and $+z$ goes away from the viewer. Front faces of the generated meshes appear clockwise on the screen, as in Direct3D. Everything in `geometry3d` is consistent with this reading; a scene modelled for a right-handed system appears mirrored left to right."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:65
msgid "Perspective projection"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:67
msgid "A pinhole camera at the origin, looking along $+z$, forms the image of the point $(x, y, z)$ on a plane at distance $\\varphi$ in front of the pinhole. By similar triangles the image is at"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:73
msgid "`PerspectiveProjection::project_point` scales this by pixels per unit, moves the origin to the centre of a $W \\times H$ viewport, and flips $y$ so that it grows downwards as screen rows do. Absorbing $\\varphi$ and the pixel density into one scale $s$:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:80
msgid "In homogeneous coordinates this is the intrinsic matrix $K$ followed by the perspective divide:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:89
msgid "The usual graphics pipeline splits $K$ into a projection to normalized device coordinates $[-1, 1]^2$ and a separate viewport transform. `view` fuses the two, because no stage in between needs normalized coordinates. It also keeps the camera-space $z$ as the depth instead of a normalized depth. A normalized depth of the form $a + b/z$ is a monotonic function of $z$, so both give the same depth-test results. Keeping $z$ makes depth values readable in world units and lets the rasterizer interpolate $1/z$ directly (below)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:91
msgid "Orthographic projection"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:93
msgid "Dropping the division gives the parallel projection $x_s = W/2 + s x$, $y_s = H/2 - s y$, with $s$ now in pixels per world unit. Sizes no longer shrink with distance. `OrthographicProjection` exists for points and for diagrams; the frontend does not rasterize with it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:95
msgid "Perspective-correct depth"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:97
msgid "A rasterizer knows the projected vertices $p_0, p_1, p_2$ and, for each pixel, the screen-space barycentric coordinates $\\lambda_i$ with $\\sum \\lambda_i = 1$ and $p = \\sum \\lambda_i p_i$. It needs the depth $z$ of the 3D point that projects to that pixel. Interpolating $z$ linearly with $\\lambda$ is wrong, because projection does not preserve ratios along a line. The correct rule is:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:99
msgid "On a projected triangle, $1/z$ is an affine function of the screen position, so $\\dfrac{1}{z} = \\displaystyle\\sum_i \\frac{\\lambda_i}{z_i}$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:101
msgid "Derivation. Let $P = \\sum \\mu_i P_i$ be the 3D point, with 3D barycentric coordinates $\\mu_i$ ($\\sum \\mu_i = 1$), so its depth is $z = \\sum \\mu_i z_i$. Measure screen positions from the centre, $\\tilde x_s = x_s - W/2$. Then"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:111
msgid "and the same computation holds for $y$. The weights $\\mu_i z_i / z$ sum to $\\big(\\sum \\mu_i z_i\\big)/z = 1$, so they are the screen-space barycentric coordinates of $p$, which are unique for a non-degenerate triangle:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:117
msgid "Dividing by $z_i$ and summing,"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:123
msgid "`interpolate_perspective_depth` evaluates exactly this. The error of linear interpolation can be large: on an edge from depth $1$ to depth $3$, the screen midpoint has true depth $(\\tfrac12 \\cdot 1 + \\tfrac12 \\cdot \\tfrac13)^{-1} = 1.5$, while the linear average says $2$. Between two intersecting or nearly touching surfaces, that difference decides which one is visible, and a test in the repository checks that the depth buffer uses the correct value."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:125
msgid "Three remarks follow from the derivation:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:127
msgid "Any affine change of screen coordinates leaves the $\\lambda_i$ unchanged, since barycentric coordinates are affine invariants. The terminal $y$ scaling of the TUI backend is such a map, so the rule stays exact after it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:128
msgid "For an orthographic projection $z$ itself is affine in the screen position, and the $1/z$ rule would not be exact; this is why the frontend uses linear depth in its (orthographic) shadow map and never feeds orthographic triangles to these rasterizers."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:129
msgid "The rule needs $z_i > 0$. When a vertex is at or behind the eye, the code falls back to linear interpolation, which is at least finite; the image is wrong in that case anyway because nothing is clipped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:131
msgid "The physical camera"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:133
msgid "A real camera with focal length $f$ (mm) and a sensor of height $h$ (mm) forms the image of $(x, y, z)$ at height $f\\,y/z$ mm on the sensor, by the pinhole formula with $\\varphi = f$. If the sensor height is shown on $H$ rows of the viewport, there are $H/h$ pixels per millimetre, so"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:141
msgid "which is `ScientificCamera::projection_scale`. The angle of view across a sensor dimension $d$ follows from the right triangle formed by the pinhole, the sensor centre and the sensor edge:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:149
msgid "the formula of `LensSpec::horizontal_fov`, `vertical_fov` and `diagonal_fov`. With this scale, a point on the edge of the vertical field ($y/z = \\tan(\\theta_h/2) = h/2f$) lands at $y_s = H/2 - s\\,h/2f = 0$, the top row: the vertical angle of view spans the viewport height exactly. Horizontally the viewport spans $2\\arctan\\!\\big(\\tfrac{W}{H} \\cdot \\tfrac{h}{2f}\\big)$, which equals the sensor's horizontal field only when $W/H = w/h$. A 640 × 480 canvas (4:3) with a full-frame sensor (3:2) therefore shows a slightly narrower horizontal field than the lens would."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:151
msgid "Units cancel in $s$: $f$ and $h$ are both millimetres, and $x/z$ is a ratio of world lengths. Scaling the whole scene and the camera distance by the same factor leaves the image unchanged, so `WorldUnit` does not enter the projection. It is used by `focal_length_world_units` and `sensor_height_world_units`, which express the optics in scene units for callers that want them."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:155
msgid "An object of height $Y$ at distance $d$ appears $s\\,Y/d = H f Y / (h d)$ pixels tall. Keeping it the same size while the camera moves requires $f/d$ to be constant, that is $f(d) = f_0\\, d / d_0$. Objects at other distances $d + \\Delta$ then change size by the factor $\\frac{d}{d + \\Delta} \\cdot \\frac{d_0 + \\Delta}{d_0}$, which is the \"vertigo\" effect the `demo` packages animate with `ScientificCamera::with_lens`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:159
msgid "A look-at camera with a derived frame"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:161
msgid "The problem: callers should be able to aim a camera without computing an orthonormal frame. A look-at description $(e, t, a)$ is what people think in, and the Gram–Schmidt-like construction above turns it into a rigid transform. Storing the three vectors (and not the matrix) keeps `Camera3` easy to animate: move `eye`, keep `target`. The cost is that each `world_to_camera_*` call rebuilds the frame; the frontend calls it per vertex, which is acceptable at demo scale."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:163
msgid "Fusing projection and viewport, keeping camera depth"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:165
msgid "Normalized device coordinates exist so that a GPU can clip and map to any framebuffer. `geometry3d` has no clipper and every backend draws in viewport units, so a separate NDC stage would add a step and no capability. Keeping $z$ as the depth makes the depth buffer hold distances along the view axis, and turns the perspective-correct rule into the short formula above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:167
msgid "Physical parameters for perspective"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:169
msgid "A field-of-view number hides two things that photographers control separately: the lens and the sensor. `ScientificCamera` takes both, which makes a dolly zoom a matter of changing one focal length, and makes the scale follow from first principles. The scale is tied to the viewport *height* so that a vertical field of view, the convention of most cameras and graphics APIs, is preserved when the viewport width changes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:171
msgid "Lenient constructors"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:173
msgid "Sensor, lens and world-unit constructors replace non-positive values by `1.0` instead of returning an error. These values come from code, not from users, and a visible but wrong picture is easier to debug in a renderer than an error path in every frame. `Viewport::new` and `Camera3::look_at` do no validation at all."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:177
msgid "$(r, u, f)$ is orthonormal with $r \\times u = f$ whenever `up` is not parallel to the viewing direction."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:178
msgid "`view_transform` is a rigid motion: it maps the eye to the origin and the target to $(0, 0, \\lVert t - e \\rVert)$, and preserves distances and dot products."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:179
msgid "For $z > 0$, `PerspectiveProjection::project_point` agrees with $K$ followed by the perspective divide; `depth` is the camera-space $z$, so depth comparisons are comparisons of distance along the view axis."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:180
msgid "`interpolate_perspective_depth` returns the exact depth of the 3D point under a pixel (up to rounding) when all three vertex depths exceed `DEPTH_EPSILON`, as derived above."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:181
msgid "`projection_scale` maps the vertical angle of view onto the viewport height: $s \\tan(\\theta_h/2) = H/2$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:182
msgid "All functions are constant time; the batch projection functions are linear in the number of points."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:186
msgid "**A 4×4 projection matrix applied with `Transform3`.** It would work (`apply_point` divides by $w$), but would replace the depth by a normalized value and require a viewport step afterwards; the three explicit formulas are clearer."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:187
msgid "**Clipping against a near plane.** It is the correct way to handle geometry that crosses the eye plane, but it splits triangles into polygons and needs a clipper in every path. The demos keep all geometry in front of the camera instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:188
msgid "**A field-of-view parameter.** It is derivable from `LensSpec` and `SensorSpec`, but cannot express a sensor change or a dolly zoom as directly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:192
msgid "`view` does not:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:194
msgid "clip geometry, define near or far planes, or handle points at or behind the eye;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:195
msgid "unproject screen points back into rays;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:196
msgid "model depth of field, lens distortion, exposure or a non-centred principal point;"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:197
msgid "correct for non-square pixels or terminal cells (the TUI backend does that);"
msgstr ""

#: manual/design/view.md:198
msgid "rasterize, shade or own any output format."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:1
msgid "geometry3d"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:3
msgid "This manual documents version `0.5.1` of `Luna-Flow/geometry3d` as it stands on `main` after its migration to MoonBit 0.10."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:5
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:7
msgid "`Luna-Flow/geometry3d` is a small 3D rendering pipeline for MoonBit, built on `Luna-Flow/linear-algebra`. It takes quad meshes through homogeneous transforms, a look-at camera with a physical lens model, perspective projection, back-face culling, Lambert shading and shadow mapping, and draws the result in a terminal, on an HTML canvas or as animated SVG. It is deliberately not a game engine: every stage is short enough to read and is documented with the mathematics it implements."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:9
msgid "Points are $(p, 1)$ and directions $(d, 0)$ in homogeneous coordinates; transforms are 4×4 matrices composed in application order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:10
msgid "Camera space has $x$ right, $y$ up and $z$ forward (the left-handed convention of Direct3D), and depth is the camera-space $z$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:11
msgid "The frontend hands every backend the same list of projected, shaded triangles; the terminal and canvas backends resolve occlusion with a depth buffer, the SVG backend by drawing order."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:13
msgid "[!WARNING] `cylinder_mesh`, `cone_mesh` and `triangular_pyramid_mesh` currently wind their faces inwards, so these solids render inside out. The [core API](api/core.md#cube_mesh-sphere_mesh-cylinder_mesh-cone_mesh-triangular_pyramid_mesh-torus_mesh) explains the effect and a workaround."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:16
msgid "Install"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:23
msgid "Then import the packages you use in your `moon.pkg`:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:35
msgid "`linear-algebra` is needed in your own module only if you name its types, for example `@la.Vector[Double]` in a signature. The browser backends also need `moonbit-community/rabbita@0.12.4`. The module requires MoonBit with `moonc` 0.10 or later. `core`, `view`, `frontend` and `backend/tui` build on every target (`wasm-gc`, `wasm`, `js`, `native`). `backend/canvas`, `backend/gsap`, `demo_canvas` and `demo_gsap` build only for `js`, and the terminal demo is meant for `native`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:37
msgid "Pages"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:39
msgid "The library packages form a chain: each depends only on the ones above it. The executables at the bottom are demos; their API pages describe a command line or an HTML page contract instead of MoonBit items. Package paths are `Luna-Flow/geometry3d/<package>`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:41
msgid "Part"
msgstr ""

#: manual/index.md:41
