msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:81
msgid "Place an object in the world"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:83
msgid "A model transform usually scales, then rotates, then translates. `compose` takes the next step as its argument, so the chain reads in that order:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:101
msgid "Tell points from directions"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:103
msgid "A direction, such as a light ray or a velocity, must not move when the object is translated. Use `apply_direction` for it:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:115
msgid "Shade the visible faces"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:117
msgid "`face_normal` gives the outward unit normal, `face_is_visible` the back-face test, and `face_intensity` the Lambert term for a light direction. The light direction points from the surface towards the light and must be a unit vector:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:147
msgid "The eye above the cube and in front of it sees the front and the top face; a light from the same quadrant lights both at $\\cos 45° \\approx 71\\%$."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:149
msgid "Triangulate for a rasterizer"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:151
msgid "Rasterizers draw triangles. `triangulate_quad` splits a quad into a fan that keeps its winding:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:171
msgid "Bring your own matrix"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:173
msgid "`Transform3::from_matrix` accepts any 4×4 `@la.Matrix[Double]`, including matrices built with `linear-algebra` operations and projective matrices. `apply_point` performs the homogeneous division, so a matrix whose last row copies $z$ into $w$ divides by depth:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:197
msgid "Hand the result to the rest of the pipeline"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:199
msgid "`core` stops at world space. The `frontend` package takes a `Mesh` and a `Transform3` per object in a `SceneObject`, and does the camera transform, projection, culling and shading for you; see the [frontend tutorial](frontend.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:203
msgid "Every vector operation allocates a new `@la.Vector[Double]`, and `apply_mesh` allocates one per vertex. That is fine for the few thousand vertices of the demos. For large meshes, transform once per frame and reuse the result rather than calling `apply_point` repeatedly on the same vertices."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:207
msgid "**Composition order.** `a.compose(b)` applies `a` first. The matrix is `b.matrix * a.matrix`, which looks reversed if you think in matrix products."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:208
msgid "**Two different lengths.** `@core.vec_length(v)` is the Euclidean norm; `v.length()` from `linear-algebra` is the number of components."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:209
msgid "**Radians.** All angles are in radians. `rotation_matrix(α, β, γ)` applies $x$, then $y$, then $z$ about world axes, and loses a degree of freedom at $\\beta = \\pm 90°$ (gimbal lock)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:210
msgid "**Light direction.** `face_intensity` expects the unit vector from the surface towards the light. A vector pointing the other way lights the back of the object; an unnormalized vector gives intensities above 1."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:211
msgid "**Mirror transforms.** A scale with an odd number of negative factors turns faces inside out: their normals point inwards, and the back-face test hides the wrong side."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:212
msgid "**Inward cylinders, cones and pyramids.** `cylinder_mesh`, `cone_mesh` and `triangular_pyramid_mesh` currently wind their faces inwards, so they render inside out. Mirror them once with `Transform3::scale(1.0, 1.0, -1.0)` (cylinder, cone) or `Transform3::scale(-1.0, 1.0, 1.0)` (pyramid) before use; see the [core API](../api/core.md#cube_mesh-sphere_mesh-cylinder_mesh-cone_mesh-triangular_pyramid_mesh-torus_mesh)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:213
msgid "**Building meshes by hand.** The fields of `Mesh` and `QuadFace` are read-only outside `core`, so a record literal such as `{ vertices, faces }` does not compile in your package. Start from a generator and reshape it with transforms."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:214
msgid "**Absolute tolerance.** `DEPTH_EPSILON` is $10^{-9}$ in world units. Scenes far larger or smaller than unit scale may hit it unexpectedly."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:218
msgid "The [core API](../api/core.md) lists every function with its exact signature."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:219
msgid "The [core design](../design/core.md) derives homogeneous transforms, the Euler-angle matrix, face orientation and Lambert shading."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:220
msgid "Continue with the [view tutorial](view.md) to put a camera in front of your meshes."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/core.md:221
msgid "`Luna-Flow/linear-algebra` documents the vector and matrix types: <https://lunaflow.cn/en/linear-algebra/>."
msgstr ""

